= #text("An Outline of Intellectual Rubbish / 论智识垃圾")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("Man is a rational animal: so at least I have been told. Throughout a long life, I have looked diligently for evidence in favour of this statement, but so far I have not had the good fortune to come across it, though I have searched in many countries spread over three continents. On the contrary, I have seen the world plunging continually further into madness. I have seen great nations, formerly leaders of civilisation, led astray by preachers of bombastic nonsense. I have seen cruelty, persecution, and superstition increasing by leaps and bounds, until we have almost reached the point where praise of rationality is held to mark a man as an old fogey regrettably surviving from a bygone age. All this is depressing, but gloom is a useless emotion. In order to escape from it, I have been driven to study the past with more attention than I had formerly given to it, and have found, as Erasmus found, that folly is perennial and yet the human race has survived. The follies of our own times are easier to bear when they are seen against the background of past follies. In what follows I shall mix the sillinesses of our day with those of former centuries. Perhaps the result may help in seeing our own times in perspective, and as not much worse than other ages that our ancestors lived through without ultimate disaster.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("人是理性的动物——至少别人是这么告诉我的。在漫长的一生中，我一直认真寻找支持这个说法的证据；尽管找遍了横跨三大洲的许多国家，至今却还没有这份好运。相反，我看见世界不断陷入更深的疯狂。我看见那些曾经引领文明的伟大民族，被鼓吹浮夸谬论的人带上歧途。我看见残酷、迫害和迷信日益猖獗，几乎到了这样的地步：赞美理性，反倒会使人被看作一个从逝去时代侥幸活到今天、不合时宜的老古董。这一切令人沮丧，可是忧郁无济于事。为了摆脱它，我不得不比从前更用心地研究过去，结果发现，正如伊拉斯谟所发现的那样，愚蠢历久不衰，人类却仍然生存了下来。把我们时代的愚行放在过去的愚行中看，就容易忍受一些。下面，我会把今天的蠢事与往昔的蠢事穿插起来。这样做，也许有助于我们看清自己所处时代的位置，并认识到：它并不比祖先们经历过、而终于没有遭到毁灭的那些时代糟糕多少。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Aristotle, so far as I know, was the first man to proclaim explicitly that man is a rational animal. His reason for this view was one which does not now seem very impressive; it was, that some people can do sums. He thought that there are three kinds of soul: the vegetable soul, possessed by all living things, both plants and animals, and concerned only with nourishment and growth; the animal soul, concerned with locomotion, and shared by man with the lower animals; and finally the rational soul, or intellect, which is the Divine mind, but in which men participate to a greater or less degree in proportion to their wisdom. It is in virtue of the intellect that man is a rational animal. The intellect is shown in various ways, but most emphatically by mastery of arithmetic. The Greek system of numerals was very bad, so that the multiplication table was quite difficult, andcomplicated calculations could only be made by very clever people. Now-adays, however, calculating machines do sums better than even the cleverest people, yet no one contends that these useful instruments are immortal, or work by divine inspiration. As arithmetic has grown easier, it has come to be less respected. The consequence is that, though many philosophers continue to tell us what fine fellows we are, it is no longer on account of our arithmetical skill that they praise us.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("据我所知，亚里士多德是第一个明确宣称人是理性动物的人。他这种观点的理由现在看来并不令人印象深刻; 是的，有些人可以做总和。他认为有三种灵魂: 植物的灵魂，拥有所有的生物，植物和动物，只关心营养和生长; 动物的灵魂，关心运动，和人类与低等动物共享; 最后是理性的灵魂，或智力，这是神圣的思想，但在其中，人们或多或少地参与他们的智慧。正是凭借理智，人才是理性的动物。智力以各种方式表现出来，但最突出的是对算术的掌握。希腊的数制非常糟糕，所以乘法表非常困难，复杂的计算只能由非常聪明的人来完成。然而，如今，计算机器的计算能力甚至比最聪明的人都要好，但没有人认为这些有用的工具是不朽的，或者是神圣灵感的作用。随着算术变得越来越容易，它越来越不受尊重。结果是，尽管许多哲学家继续告诉我们我们是多么优秀的人，但他们赞美我们不再是因为我们的算术技能。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("Bertrand Russell Quotes 366")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("Bertrand Russell引用366")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("Since the fashion of the age no longer allows us to point to calculating boys as evidence that man is rational and the soul, at least in part, immortal, let us look elsewhere. Where shall we look first? Shall we look among eminent statesmen, who have so triumphantly guided the world into its present condition? Or shall we choose the men of letters? Or the philosophers? All these have their claims, but 1 think we should begin with those whom all right thinking people acknowledge to be the wisest as well as the best of men, namely the clergy. If they fail to be rational, what hope is there for us lesser mortals? And alas-though I say it with all due respect-there have been times when their wisdom has not been very obvious, and, strange to say, these were especially the times when the power of the clergy was greatest.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("由于这个时代的时尚不再允许我们把精打细算的男孩作为证据，证明人是理性的，灵魂至少在某种程度上是不朽的，让我们看看其他地方。我们先从哪里看起呢？我们应该看看杰出的政治家，他们如此胜利地引导世界进入目前的状况吗？还是我们应该选择文人？或者哲学家们？所有这些都有自己的主张，但我认为我们应该从那些认为自己是最聪明的人开始，也就是神职人员。如果他们不理性，我们这些小凡人还有什么希望？唉 -- 虽然我说得很尊重 -- 有些时候他们的智慧不是很明显，而且，奇怪的是，这些尤其是神职人员的权力最大的时候。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("The Ages of Faith, which are praised by our neo-scholastics, were the time when the clergy had things all their own way. Daily life was full of miracles wrought by saints and wizardry perpetrated by devils and necromancers. Many thousands of witches were burnt at the stake. Men's sins were punished by pestilence and famine, by earthquake, flood, and fire. And yet, strange to say, they were even more sinful than they are now-a-days. Very little was known scientifically about the world. A few learned men remembered Greek proofs that the earth is round, but most people made fun of the notion that there are antipodes. To suppose that there are human beings at the antipodes was heresy. It was generally held (though modern Catholics take a milder view) that the immense majority of mankind are damned. Dangers were held to lurk at every turn. Devils would settle on the food that monks were about to eat, and would take possession of the bodies of incautious feeders who omitted to make the sign of the Cross before each mouthful. Old-fashioned people still say \"bless you\" when one sneezes, but they have forgotten the reason for the custom. The reason was that people were thought to sneeze out their souls, and before their souls could get back lurking demons were apt to enter the unsouled body; but if any one said \"God bless you,\" the demons were frightened off.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("被我们的新经院哲学家们所称颂的信仰时代，正是教士们一手掌控一切的时期。日常生活充满了由圣徒创造的奇迹，也充斥着由恶魔与死灵法师施展的巫术。成千上万的女巫被处以火刑。人类的罪恶遭到了瘟疫与饥荒、地震、洪水以及火灾的惩罚。然而，说来也怪，他们那时的罪恶甚至比如今还要深重。人们对这个世界在科学上知之甚少。一些学识渊博的人还记得古希腊人关于地球是圆的证明，但大多数人却嘲笑存在“对跖点”这一说法。认为在地球另一端有人类存在，这在当时被视为异端。人们普遍认为（尽管现代天主教徒持较为温和的观点），绝大多数人类都将遭受谴责。人们认为，危险无处不在。魔鬼会选择僧侣要吃的食物，并会占有不谨慎的饲养者的尸体，这些饲养者在每口之前都没有做十字架的标志。老派的人在别人打喷嚏时仍会说“保重”，但他们已忘记了这一习俗的由来。原因是人们被认为打喷嚏了他们的灵魂，在他们的灵魂回来之前，潜伏的恶魔很容易进入没有灵魂的身体; 但是如果有人说 “上帝保佑你”，恶魔就被吓跑了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("Throughout the last 400 years, during which the growth of science had gradually shown men how to acquire knowledge of the ways of nature and mastery over natural forces, the clergy have fought a losing battle against science, in astronomy and geology, in anatomy and physiology, in biology and psychology and sociology. Ousted from one position, they have taken up another. After being worsted in astronomy, they did their best to prevent the rise of geology; they fought against Darwin in biology, and at the present time they fight against scientific theories of psychology and education. At each stage, they try to make the public forget their earlier obscurantism, in order that their present obscurantism may not be recognized for what it is. Let us note a few instances of irrationality among the clergy since the rise of science, and then inquire whether the rest of mankind are any better.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("在过去的400年里，科学的发展逐渐向人们展示了如何获得自然方式的知识和对自然力量的掌握，神职人员在天文学和地质学、解剖学和生理学、生物学、心理学和社会学方面与科学进行了一场失败的战斗。被免去一个职务后，他们又担任了另一个职务。在被天文学精打之后，他们竭尽全力阻止地质学的兴起; 他们在生物学上与达尔文作斗争，目前他们与心理学和教育学的科学理论作斗争。在每个阶段，他们都试图让公众忘记他们早期的蒙昧主义，以便他们目前的蒙昧主义可能不会被认可。让我们列举一些自科学兴起以来教士群体中表现出的非理性现象，然后探讨其余人类是否更为理性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("When Benjamin Franklin invented the lightning rod, the clergy, both in England and America, with the enthusiastic support of George III, condemned it as an impious attempt to defeat the will of God. For, as all rightthinking people were aware, lightning is sent by God to punish impiety or some other grave sin-the virtuous are never struck by lightning. Therefore if God wants to strike any one, Benjamin Franklin ought not to defeat His design; indeed, to do so is helping criminals to escape. But God was equal to the occasion, if we are to believe the eminent Dr. Price, one of the leading divines of Boston. Lightning having been rendered ineffectual by the \"iron points invented by the sagacious Dr. Franklin,\" Massachusetts was shaken by earthquakes, which Dr. Price perceived to be due to God's wrath at the \"iron points.\" In a sermon on the subject he said, \"In Boston are more erected than elsewhere in New England, and Boston seems to be more dreadfully shaken. Oh! there is no getting out of the mighty hand of God.\" Apparently, however, Providence gave up all hope of curing Boston of its wickedness, for, though lightning rods became more and more common, earthquakes in Massachusetts have remained rare. Nevertheless, Dr. Price's point of view, or something very like it, is still held by one of the most influential of living men. When, at one time, there were several bad earthquakes in India, Mahatma Gandhi solemnly warned his compatriots that these disasters had been sent as a punishment for their sins.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("当本杰明·富兰克林发明避雷针时，英国和美国的神职人员在乔治三世的热情支持下，谴责它是一种不虔诚的企图，企图打败上帝的意志。因为，正如所有有正确思想的人都知道的那样，闪电是上帝用来惩罚不虔诚或其他严重的罪恶的-善良的人永远不会被闪电击中。因此，如果上帝想打击任何人，本杰明·富兰克林不应该打败他的设计; 事实上，这样做是帮助罪犯逃脱。但是，如果我们要相信著名的普莱斯博士，波士顿的主要神灵之一，上帝就等于这个场合。闪电因 “睿智的富兰克林博士发明的铁点” 而变得无效，马萨诸塞州因地震而动摇，普莱斯博士认为这是由于上帝对 “铁点” 的愤怒。在关于这个主题的布道中，他说: “在波士顿，比在新英格兰的其他地方更加竖立，波士顿似乎更加可怕地动摇了。哦!没有摆脱上帝的大能之手。”然而，显然，普罗维登斯放弃了治愈波士顿邪恶的所有希望，因为尽管避雷针变得越来越普遍，但马萨诸塞州的地震仍然很少见。尽管如此，普莱斯博士的观点，或者非常类似的观点，仍然是最有影响力的活着的人之一。当一次印度发生几次严重地震时，圣雄甘地郑重警告他的同胞，这些灾难是对他们的罪过的惩罚。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("Even in my own native island this point of view still exists. During the last war, the British Government did much to stimulate the production of food at home. In 1916, when things were not going well, a Scottish clergyman wrote to the newspapers to say that military failure was due to the fact that, with government sanction, potatoes had been planted on the Sabbath. However, disaster was averted, owing to the fact that the Germans disobeyed all the Ten Commandments, and not only one of them.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("即使在我自己的家乡岛上，这种观点仍然存在。在上次战争期间，英国政府做了很多事情来刺激国内的粮食生产。在1916，当事情进展不顺利时，一位苏格兰神职人员写信给报纸说，军事失败是由于以下事实: 在政府的制裁下，安息日种植了土豆。然而，由于德国人不遵守所有的十诫，而不仅仅是其中的一条，灾难得以避免。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("Sometimes, if pious men are to be believed, God's mercies are curiously selective. Toplady, the author of \"Rock of Ages,\" moved from one vicarage to another; a week after the move, the vicarage he had formerly occupied burnt down, with great loss to the new vicar. Thereupon Toplady thanked God; but what the new vicar did is not known. Borrow, in his \"Bible in Spain,\" records how without mishap he crossed a mountain pass infested by bandits. The next party to cross, however, were set upon, robbed, and some of them murdered; when Borrow heard of this, he, like Toplady, thanked God.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("有时候，如果要相信虔诚的人，上帝的怜悯是奇怪的选择性。Toplady，“摇滚时代” 的作者，从一个牧师转移到另一个牧师; 搬家一周后，他以前占领的牧师被烧毁了，给新牧师带来了巨大的损失。于是Toplady感谢上帝; 但是新牧师做了什么还不知道。借用，在他的 “西班牙圣经” 中，记录了他如何顺利越过土匪出没的山口。下一个要跨越的政党，然而，被设置，抢劫，他们中的一些人被谋杀了; 当借款听到这个消息时，他像Toplady一样感谢上帝。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("Although we are taught the Copernican astronomy in our textbooks, it has not yet penetrated to our religion or our morals, and has not even succeeded in destroying belief in astrology. People still think that the Divine Plan has special reference to human beings, and that a special Providence not only looks after the good, but also punishes the wicked. I am sometimes shocked by the blasphemies of those who think themselves pious-for instance, the nuns who never take a bath without wearing a bathrobe all the time. When asked why, since no man can see them, they reply: \"Oh, but you forget the good God.\" Apparently they conceive of the Deity as a Peeping Tom, whose omnipotence enables Him to see through bathroom walls, but who is foiled by bathrobes. This view strikes me as curious.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("尽管我们在教科书中被教导哥白尼天文学，但它尚未渗透到我们的宗教或道德中，甚至还没有成功地摧毁对占星术的信仰。人们仍然认为神圣的计划对人类有特殊的意义，特殊的天意不仅照顾好人，而且惩罚恶人。我有时对那些认为自己虔诚的人的亵渎行为感到震惊，例如，那些一直不穿浴袍从不洗澡的尼居们。当被问到为什么，因为没有人能看到他们，他们回答说: “哦，但是你忘记了善良的上帝。”显然，他们认为神灵是偷窥狂，他的无所不能使他能够看穿浴室的墙壁，但被浴袍挫败了。这种观点让我觉得颇为奇怪。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The whole conception of \"Sin\" is one which I find very puzzling, doubtless owing to my sinful nature. If \"Sin\" consisted in causing needless suffering, I could understand; but on the contrary, sin often consists in avoiding needless suffering. Some years ago, in the English House of Lords, a bill was introduced to legalize euthanasia in cases of painful and incurable disease. The patient's consent was to be necessary, as well as several medical certificates. To me, in my simplicity, it would seem natural to require the patient's consent, but the late Archbishop of Canterbury, the English official expert on Sin, explained the erroneousness of such a view. The patient's consent turns euthanasia into suicide, and suicide is sin. Their Lordships listened to the voice of authority, and rejected the bill. Consequently, to please the Archbishop-and his God, if he reports truly-victims of cancer still have to endure months of wholly useless agony, unless their doctors or nurses are sufficiently humane to risk a charge of murder. I find difficulty in the conception of a God who gets pleasure from contemplating such tortures; and if there were a God capable of such wanton cruelty, I should certainly not think Him worthy of worship. But that only proves how sunk I am in moral depravity.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("“罪”这个观念，整个说来都让我十分困惑；这无疑是因为我本性有罪。如果“罪”指的是造成不必要的痛苦，我倒能理解；可是，罪往往反而在于避免不必要的痛苦。几年前，英国上议院提出过一项法案，拟将针对痛苦而无可治愈的疾病实施的安乐死合法化，条件是必须得到病人同意，并取得几份医疗证明。在我这种头脑简单的人看来，要求病人同意似乎再自然不过了；但已故坎特伯雷大主教——英国官方的罪学专家——却解释了这种看法错在哪里：病人的同意会使安乐死变成自杀，而自杀是罪。诸位阁下听从了权威的声音，否决了法案。结果，为了取悦大主教，以及他的上帝——如果他对上帝的转述属实——癌症患者仍得忍受几个月毫无意义的剧痛，除非他们的医生或护士足够仁慈，愿意冒被控谋杀的危险。我很难理解一位会从观看这种折磨中获得乐趣的上帝；假如真有一位能如此肆意残酷的上帝，我当然不会认为他值得崇拜。不过，这也只能证明，我在道德堕落中陷得有多深。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("I am equally puzzled by the things that are sin and by the things that are not. When the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals asked the pope for his support, he refused it, on the ground that human beings owe no duty to the lower animals, and that ill-treating animals is not sinful. This is because animals have no souls. On the other hand, it is wicked to marry your deceased wife's sister-so at least the Church teaches-however much you and she may wish to marry. This is not because of any unhappiness that might result, but because of certain texts in the Bible.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("哪些事算罪、哪些事不算罪，同样让我困惑。防止虐待动物协会请求教皇支持时，他拒绝了，理由是人类对低等动物并不负有义务，虐待动物也不构成罪，因为动物没有灵魂。另一方面，娶亡妻的姐妹却是邪恶的——至少教会是这么教导的——不管你和她多么希望结婚。这不是因为婚姻可能造成什么不幸，而是因为《圣经》里的某些经文。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("The resurrection of the body, which is an article of the Apostles' Creed, is a dogma which has various curious consequences. There was an author not very many years ago, who had an ingenious method of calculating the date of the end of the world. He argued that there must be enough of the necessary ingredients of a human body to provide everybody with the requisites at the Last Day. By carefully calculating the available raw material, he decided that it would all have been used up by a certain date. When that date comes, the world must end, since otherwise the resurrection of the body would become impossible. Unfortunately I have forgotten what the date was, but I believe it is not very distant.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("身体的复活，这是使徒信经的一篇文章，是一个有各种奇怪后果的教条。不是很多年前，有一位作家，他有一种巧妙的方法来计算世界末日的日期。他认为，人体必须有足够的必要成分，才能在最后一天为每个人提供必需品。通过仔细计算可用的原材料，他决定在某个日期之前全部用完。当那个日期到来时，世界必须结束，因为否则身体的复活将变得不可能。不幸的是，我已经忘记了日期是什么，但我相信它不是很遥远。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("St. Thomas Aquinas, the official philosopher of the Catholic Church, discussed lengthily and seriously a very grave problem, which, I fear, modern theologians unduly neglect. He imagines a cannibal who has never eaten anything but human flesh, and whose father and mother before him had like propensities. Every particle of his body belongs rightfully to someone else. We cannot suppose that those who have been eaten by cannibals are to go short through all eternity. But, if not, what is left for the cannibal? How is he to be properly roasted in hell, if all his body is restored to its original owners? This is a puzzling question, as the Saint rightly perceives.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("圣托马斯·阿奎那，天主教会的官方哲学家，讨论冗长和严重的一个非常严重的问题，哪一个，我担心，现代神学家过度忽视。他想象一个食人族，除了人肉，他从来没有吃过任何东西，在他之前，他的父亲和母亲都有类似的倾向。他身体的每一个粒子都理所当然地属于别人。我们不能假设那些被食人族吃掉的人会永远短缺。但是，如果没有，食人族还剩下什么？如果他所有的身体都恢复了原来的主人，他如何在地狱中被正确地烤熟？这是一个令人费解的问题，正如圣人正确地感知。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("In this connection the orthodox have a curious objection to cremation, which seems to show an insufficient realization of God's omnipotence. It is thought that a body which has been burnt will be more difficult for Him to collect together again than one which has been put underground and transformed into worms. No doubt collecting the particles from the air and undoing the chemical work of combustion would be somewhat laborious, but it is surely blasphemous to suppose such a work impossible for the Deity. I conclude that the objection to cremation implies grave heresy. But I doubt whether my opinion will carry much weight with the orthodox.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("在这方面，东正教对火葬有一个奇怪的反对意见，这似乎表明对上帝无所不能的认识不足。人们认为，被烧毁的尸体比被放在地下并变成蠕虫的尸体更难重新收集起来。毫无疑问，从空气中收集颗粒并取消燃烧的化学工作会有些费力，但是，假设这样的工作对神灵来说是不可能的，这无疑是亵渎神明的。我的结论是，反对火葬意味着严重的异端。但我怀疑我的观点是否会对东正教有多大影响。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("It was only very slowly and reluctantly that the Church sanctioned the dissection of corpses in connection with the study of medicine. The pioneer in dissection was Vesalius, who was Court physician to the Emperor Charles V. His medical skill led the emperor to protect him, but after the emperor was dead he got into trouble. A corpse which he was dissecting was said to have shown signs of life under the knife, and he was accused of murder. The Inquisition was induced by King Phillip II to take a lenient view, and only sentenced him to a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. On the way home he was shipwrecked and died of exhaustion. For centuries after this time, medical students at the Papal University in Rome were only allowed to operate on lay figures, from which the sexual parts were omitted.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("教会只是非常缓慢和不情愿地批准了与医学研究有关的尸体解剖。解剖的先驱是维萨里，他是查理五世皇帝的宫廷医生。他的医术使皇帝保护了他，但是皇帝死后，他遇到了麻烦。据说他正在解剖的一具尸体在刀下显示出生命迹象，他被指控谋杀。国王菲利普二世诱使宗教裁判所采取了宽大的观点，只判他朝圣圣地。在回家的路上，他遇难了，筋疲力尽。在此之后的几个世纪中，罗马教皇大学的医学生只被允许对外行人物进行手术，而性部分被省略了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("The sacredness of corpses is a widespread belief. It was carried furthest by the Egyptians, among whom it led to the practice of mummification. It still exists in full force in China. A French surgeon, who was employed by the Chinese to teach Western medicine, relates that his demand for corpses to dissect was received with horror, but he was assured that he could have instead an unlimited supply of live criminals. His objection to this alternative was totally unintelligible to his Chinese employers.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("尸体的神圣性是一种普遍的信仰。它被埃及人带到最远的地方，其中导致了木乃伊化的实践。它在中国仍然存在。一位受雇于中国人教授西医的法国外科医生说，他对解剖尸体的要求令人震惊，但他得到保证，他可以无限制地提供活罪犯。他对这种选择的反对对他的中国雇主来说是完全难以理解的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("Although there are many kinds of sin, seven of which are deadly, the most fruitful field for Satan's wiles is sex. The orthodox Catholic doctrine on this subject is to be found in St. Paul, St. Augustine, and St. Thomas Aquinas. It is best to be celibate, but those who have not the gift of continence may marry. Intercourse in marriage is not sin, provided it is motivated by desire for offspring. All intercourse outside marriage is sin, and so is intercourse within marriage if any measures are adopted to prevent conception. Interruption of pregnancy is sin, even if, in medical opinion, it is the only way of saving the mother's life; for medical opinion is fallible, and God can always save a life by miracle if He sees fit. (This view is embodied in the law of Connecticut.) Venereal disease is God's punishment for sin. It is true that, through a guilty husband, this punishment may fall on an innocent woman and her children, but this is a mysterious dispensation of Providence, which it would be impious to question. We must also not inquire why venereal disease was not divinely instituted until the time of Columbus. Since it is the appointed penalty for sin, all measures for its avoidance are also sin-except, of course, a virtuous life. Marriage is nominally indissoluble, but many people who seem to be married are not. In the case of influential Catholics, some ground for nullity can often be found, but for the poor there is no such outlet, except perhaps in cases of impotence. Persons who divorce and remarry are guilty of adultery in the sight of God.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("尽管有许多种罪，其中七种是致命的，但撒旦诡计多义的最富有成果的领域是性。关于这个问题的东正教天主教教义是在圣保罗，圣奥古斯丁和圣托马斯·阿奎那发现。最好是独身，但那些没有节制天赋的人可以结婚。婚姻中的性交不是罪，只要它是出于对后代的渴望。婚姻之外的所有性交都是罪恶，如果采取任何措施防止受孕，婚姻内的性交也是罪恶。中断怀孕是一种罪，即使在医学上认为这是挽救母亲生命的唯一方法; 因为医学上的观点是错误的，如果上帝认为合适，他总是可以通过奇迹来挽救生命。(这种观点体现在康涅狄格州的法律中。)性病是上帝对罪的惩罚。诚然，通过一个有罪的丈夫，这种惩罚可能落在一个无辜的女人和她的孩子身上，但这是一个神秘的天意，这将是不虔诚的问题。我们也不能询问为什么直到哥伦布时代才神圣地制定了性病。因为它是对罪的指定惩罚，所有避免它的措施也是罪-当然，除了一个良性的生活。婚姻名义上是不可分割的，但许多似乎结婚的人却不是。在有影响力的天主教徒的情况下，往往可以找到一些无效的理由，但对于穷人来说，没有这样的出路，也许除了在无能的情况下。离婚和再婚的人在上帝面前犯了通奸罪。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("The phrase \"in the sight of God\" puzzles me. One would suppose that God sees everything, but apparently this is a mistake. He does not see Reno, for you cannot be divorced in the sight of God. Registry offices are a doubtful point. I notice that respectable people, who would not call on anybody who lives in open sin, are quite willing to call on people who have had only a civil marriage; so apparently God does see registry offices.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("“在上帝面前” 这句话让我困惑。有人会认为上帝看到了一切，但显然这是一个错误。他看不见里诺，因为你不能在上帝面前离婚。登记处是一个值得怀疑的问题。我注意到那些受人尊敬的人，他们不会拜访任何生活在公开罪恶中的人，他们非常愿意拜访那些只有过民事婚姻的人; 显然上帝确实看到了登记处。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("Some eminent men think even the doctrine of the Catholic Church deplorably lax where sex is concerned. Tolstoy and Mahatma Gandhi, in their old age, laid it down that all sexual intercourse is wicked, even in marriage and with a view to offspring. The Manicheans thought likewise, relying upon men's native sinfulness to supply them with a continually fresh crop of disciples. This doctrine, however, is heretical, though it is equally heretical to maintain that marriage is as praiseworthy as celibacy. Tolstoy thinks tobacco almost as bad as sex; in one of his novels, a man who is contemplating murder smokes a cigarette first in order to generate the necessary homicidal fury. Tobacco, however, is not prohibited in the Scriptures, though, as Samuel Butler points at, St. Paul would no doubt have denounced it if he had known of it.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("一些杰出的人甚至认为天主教会的教义在涉及性方面也令人遗憾。托尔斯泰和圣雄甘地，在他们年老的时候，规定所有的性交都是邪恶的，即使是在婚姻中，也是为了后代。摩尼教人也有同样的想法，依靠男人的原罪来为他们提供不断新鲜的门徒。然而，这种学说是异端的，尽管坚持婚姻与独身生活一样值得称赞同样是异端的。托尔斯泰认为烟草几乎和性一样糟糕; 在他的一部小说中，一个正在考虑谋杀的人首先抽烟，以产生必要的杀人愤怒。然而，烟草在圣经中是不被禁止的，但是，正如塞缪尔·巴特勒指出的那样，圣保罗无疑会谴责它，如果他知道的话。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("It is odd that neither the Church nor modern public opinion condemns petting, provided it stops short at a certain point. At what point sin begins is a matter as to which casuists differ. One eminently orthodox Catholic divine laid it down that a confessor may fondle a nun's breasts, provided he does it without evil intent. But I doubt whether modern authorities would agree with him on this point.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("奇怪的是，教会和现代公众舆论都不谴责抚摸，只要它在某个时候停止。罪恶在什么时候开始是一个不同的问题。一位杰出的东正教天主教神规定，悔者可以抚摸修女的乳房，前提是他没有邪恶的意图。但我怀疑现代当局是否会在这一点上同意他的观点。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("Modern morals are a mixture of two elements: on the one hand, rational precepta as to how to live together peaceably in a society, and on the other hand traditional taboos derived originally from some ancient superstition, but proximately from sacred books, Christian, Mohammedan, Hindu, or Buddhist. To some extent the two agree; the prohibition of murder and theft, for instance, is supported both by human reason and by Divine command. But the prohibition of pork or beef has only scriptural authority, and that only in certain religions. It is odd that modern men, who are aware of what science has done in the way of bringing new knowledge and altering the conditions of social life, should still be willing to accept the authority of texts embodying the outlook of very ancient and very ignorant pastoral or agricultural tribes. It is discouraging that many of the precepts whose sacred character is thus uncritically acknowledged should be such as to inflict much wholly unnecessary misery. If men's kindly impulses were stronger, they would find some way of explaining that these precepts are not to be taken literally, any more than the command to \"sell all that thou hast and give to the poor.\"")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("现代道德是两个要素的混合体: 一方面，关于如何在社会中和平共处的理性原则; 另一方面，传统禁忌最初源于一些古老的迷信，但大致来自神圣的书籍，基督教，伊斯兰教，印度教或佛教徒。在某种程度上，两者是一致的; 禁止谋杀和盗窃，例如，是由人类的理性和神圣的命令支持。但禁止猪肉或牛肉只有圣经的权威，只有在某些宗教。奇怪的是，现代人知道科学在带来新知识和改变社会生活条件方面所做的工作，却仍然愿意接受体现非常古老和非常无知的牧民或农业部落观点的文本的权威。令人沮丧的是，许多其神圣性质的戒律，因此不加批判地承认应该是这样的，以造成完全不必要的痛苦。如果男人的好心冲动更强，他们会找到某种方式来解释这些戒律不是字面上的，比 “卖掉你所有的东西给穷人” 的命令更多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("There are logical difficulties in the notion of sin. We are told that sin consists in disobedience to God's commands, but we are also told that God is omnipotent. If He is, nothing contrary to His will can occur; therefore when the sinner disobeys His commands, He must have intended this to happen. St. Augustine boldly accepts this view, and asserts that men are led to sin by a blindness with which God afflicts them. But most theologians, in modern times, have felt that, if God causes men to sin, it is not fair to send them to hell for what they cannot help. We are told that sin consists in acting contrary to God's will. This, however, does not get rid of the difficulty. Those who, like Spinoza, take God's omnipotence seriously, deduce that there can be no such thing as sin. This leads to frightful results. What! said Spinoza's contemporaries, was it not wicked of Nero to murder his mother? Was it not wicked of Adam to eat the apple? Is one action just as good as another? Spinoza wriggles, but does not find any satisfactory answer. If everything happens in accordance with God's will, God must have wanted Nero to murder his mother; therefore, since God is good, the murder must have been a good thing. From this argument there is no escape.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("罪的概念中存在着逻辑上的困难。我们被告知，罪包括不服从上帝的命令，但我们也被告知，上帝是万能的。如果他是，任何违背他的意志的事情都不会发生; 因此，当罪人不服从他的命令时，他一定是故意这样做的。圣奥古斯丁大胆地接受了这一观点，并断言人们被上帝折磨他们的盲目所导致的罪恶。但是，在现代，大多数神学家都认为，如果上帝使人犯罪，那么将他们送入地狱是不公平的，因为他们无能为力。我们被告知，罪在于违背上帝的旨意。然而，这并不能消除这一困难。那些像斯宾诺莎一样认真对待上帝全能的人，推断出没有罪这回事。这导致了可怕的后果。什么！斯宾诺莎的同时代人说，尼禄谋杀他的母亲不是邪恶的吗？亚当吃那颗苹果难道不是罪恶的吗？一个行动是否和另一个行动一样好？斯宾诺莎扭来扭去，但没有找到任何令人满意的答案。如果一切都按照上帝的旨意发生，上帝一定希望尼禄谋杀他的母亲; 因此，既然上帝是好的，谋杀一定是一件好事。从这个论点中无处可逃。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("On the other hand, those who are in earnest in thinking that sin is disobedience to God are compelled to say that God is not omnipotent. This gets out of all the logical puzzles, and is the view adopted by a certain school of liberal theologians. It has, however, its own difficulties. How are we to know what really is God's will? If the forces of evil have a certain share of power, they may deceive us into accepting as Scripture what is really their work. This was the view of the Gnostics, who thought that the Old Testament was the work of an evil spirit.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("另一方面，那些认真地认为罪是对上帝的不服从的人被迫说上帝不是万能的。这摆脱了所有的逻辑难题，是自由神学家的某个流派所采用的观点。然而，它也有其自身的困难。我们如何才能知道什么是上帝真正的旨意？如果邪恶势力有一定的权力，他们可能会欺骗我们接受圣经什么是真正的工作。这是诺斯替派的观点，他们认为旧约是恶灵的作品。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("As soon as we abandon our own reason, and are content to rely upon authority, there is no end to our troubles. Whose authority? The Old Testament? The New Testament? The Koran? In practice, people choose the book considered sacred by the community in which they are born, and out of that book they choose the parts they like, ignoring the others. At one time, the most influential text in the Bible was: \"Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live.\" Now-a-days, people pass over this text, in silence if possible; if not, with an apology. And so, even when we have a sacred book, we still choose as truth whatever suits our own prejudices. No Catholic, for instance, takes seriously the text which says that a bishop should be the husband of one wife.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("一旦我们放弃自己的理性，并满足于依靠权威，我们的麻烦就没有尽头。谁的权威？旧约圣经？新约圣经？《古兰经》？在实践中，人们选择了他们出生的社区认为神圣的书，然后从书中选择他们喜欢的部分，而忽略了其他部分。有一次，圣经中最有影响力的文字是: “你不应该让女巫活着。”现在，人们通过这段文字，如果可能的话，保持沉默; 如果没有，道歉。因此，即使我们有一本神圣的书，我们仍然选择适合我们自己偏见的任何东西作为真理。例如，没有天主教徒认真对待主教应该是一个妻子的丈夫的经文。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("People's beliefs have various causes. One is that there is some evidence for the belief in question. We apply this to matters of fact, such as \"what is so-and-so's telephone number?\" or \"who won the World Series?\" But as soon as it comes to anything more debatable, the causes of belief become less defensible. We believe, first and foremost, what makes us feel that we are fine fellows. Mr. Homo, if he has a good digestion and a sound income, thinks to himself how much more sensible he is than his neighbour so-and-so, who married a flighty wife and is always losing money. He thinks how superior his city is to the one 50 miles away: it has a bigger Chamber of Commerce and a more enterprising Rotary Club, and its mayor has never been in prison. He thinks how immeasurably his country surpasses all others. If he is an Englishman, he thinks of Shakespeare and Milton, or of Newton and Darwin, or of Nelson and Wellington, according to his temperament. If he is a Frenchman, he congratulates himself on the fact that for centuries France has led the world in culture, fashions, and cookery. If he is a Russian, he reflects that he belongs to the only nation which is truly international. If he is a Yugoslav, he boasts of his nation's pigs; if a native of the Principality of Monaco, he boasts of leading the world in the matter of gambling.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("人们的信念有多种成因。一个是有一些证据表明有问题的信念。我们将其应用于事实问题，例如 “某某的电话号码是什么？” 或 “谁赢得了世界大赛？”但是，一旦涉及到任何更具争议的事情，信仰的原因就变得不那么站得住脚了。我们首先相信，是什么让我们觉得我们是好人。Homo先生，如果他有良好的消化能力和良好的收入，他认为自己比他的邻居某个人更明智，他娶了一个轻快的妻子，总是在赔钱。他认为自己的城市比50英里外的城市优越得多: 它拥有更大的商会和更具进取心的扶轮社，而且市长从未入狱。他认为他的国家超越了所有其他国家。如果他是英国人，根据他的气质，他会想到莎士比亚和弥尔顿，或者牛顿和达尔文，或者纳尔逊和惠灵顿。如果他是法国人，他会祝贺自己几个世纪以来法国在文化，时尚和烹饪方面一直处于世界领先地位。如果他是俄罗斯人，他反映出他属于唯一真正国际化的国家。如果他是南斯拉夫人，他吹嘘自己国家的猪; 如果他是摩纳哥公国的本地人，他吹嘘自己在赌博方面领先世界。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("But these are not the only matters on which he has to congratulate himself. For is he not an individual of the species homo sapiens? Alone among animals he has an immortal soul, and is rational; he knows the difference between good and evil, and has learnt the multiplication table. Did not God make him in His own image? And was not everything created for man's convenience? The sun was made to light the day, and the moon to light the night--though the moon, by some oversight, only shines during half the nocturnal hours. The raw fruits of the earth were made for human sustenance. Even the white tails of rabbits, according to some theologians, have a purpose, namely to make it easier for sportsmen to shoot them. There are, it is true, some inconveniences: lions and tigers are too fierce, the summer is too hot, and the winter too cold. But these things only began after Adam ate the apple; before that, all animals were vegetarians, and the season was always spring. If only Adam had been content with peaches and nectarines, grapes and pears and pineapples, these blessings would still be ours.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("但这些并非他唯一可以自庆之事。难道他不是智人这一物种的个体吗？在所有动物中，唯有他拥有不朽的灵魂，并且具有理性；他懂得善恶之别，也学会了乘法表。难道上帝不是按照自己的形象造了他吗？难道万物不都是为了人类的便利而被创造的吗？太阳被造来照亮白昼，月亮被造来照亮黑夜——尽管由于某种疏忽，月亮只在夜晚的一半时间里发光。大地的天然果实本为人类所食。甚至据一些神学家所说，兔子的白色尾巴也有其用途，那就是便于猎人将其射中。诚然，也有些不便之处：狮子和老虎过于凶猛，夏天过于炎热，冬天又过于寒冷。但这些事情直到亚当吃了那颗苹果之后才开始；在那之前，所有的动物都是素食者，而季节永远是春天。要是亚当当初只满足于桃子、油桃、葡萄、梨和菠萝，这些福分至今仍会属于我们。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("Self-importance, individual or generic, is the source of most of our religious beliefs. Even sin is a conception derived from self-importance. Borrow relates how he met a Welsh preacher who was always melancholy. By sympathetic questioning he was brought to confess the source of his sorrow: that at the age of seven he had committed the sin against the Holy Ghost. \"My dear fellow,\" said Borrow, \"don't let that trouble you; I know dozens of people in like case. Do not imagine yourself cut off from the rest of mankind by this occurrence; if you inquire, you will find multitudes who suffer from the same misfortune.\" From that moment, the man was cured. He had enjoyed feeling singular, but there was no pleasure in being one of a herd of sinners. Most sinners are rather less egotistical; but theologians undoubtedly enjoy the feeling that Man is the special object of God's wrath, as well as of His love. After the Fall-so Milton assures us'__")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("无论是个人的还是普遍意义上的自大，都是我们大多数宗教信仰的根源。甚至罪恶也是一种源于自我重要感的观念。博罗讲述了他如何结识了一位总是郁郁寡欢的威尔士传教士。通过循循善诱的询问，他终于坦白了自己忧伤的根源：原来在七岁时，他曾犯下亵渎圣灵之罪。“我亲爱的朋友，” 劳拉说，“别让那麻烦你; 我认识几十个类似情况的人。不要想象自己会因为这一事件而与人类隔绝; 如果你去询问，你会发现许多人遭受同样的不幸。”从那一刻起，那人便痊愈了。他曾享受过那种独一无二的感觉，但成为一群罪人中的一员却毫无乐趣可言。大多数罪人远没有那么自我中心；然而，神学家无疑乐于感受到：人既是上帝之怒的特殊对象，也是祂之爱的特殊对象。在堕落之后——弥尔顿如此向我们保证——")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("The sun")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("太阳")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("Had first his precept so to move, so shine, As might affect the earth with cold and heat Scarce tolerable; and from the north to call Decrepit winter; from the south to bring Solstitial summer's heat.'")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("他首先发出这样的指令：使天体如此运行，如此辉耀，以致其寒热之力几令人难以忍受；自北方召唤衰老的寒冬，自南方引来至日之夏的酷暑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("However disagreeable the results may have been, Adam could hardly help feeling flattered that such vast astronomical phenomena should be brought about to teach him a lesson. The whole of theology, in regard to hell no less than to heaven, takes it for granted that Man is what is of most importance in the Universe of created beings. Since all theologians are men, this postulate has met with little opposition.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("无论结果多么令人不快，亚当都忍不住感到受宠若惊，因为这样巨大的天文现象应该给他一个教训。整个神学，关于地狱不亚于天堂，理所当然地认为人在创造的宇宙中是最重要的。由于所有神学家均为男性，这一假设几乎未遭质疑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("Since evolution became fashionable, the glorification of Man has taken a new form. We are told that evolution has been guided by one great Purpose: through the millions of years when there were only slime, or trilobites, throughout the ages of dinosaurs and giant ferns, of bees and wild flowers, God was preparing the Great Climax. At last, in the fullness of time, He produced Man, including such specimens as Nero and Caligula, Hitler and Mussolini, whose transcendent glory justified the long painful process. For my part, I find even eternal damnation less incredible, and certainly less ridiculous, than this lame and impotent conclusion which we are asked to admire as the supreme effort of Omnipotence. And if God is indeed omnipotent, why could He not have produced the glorious result without such a long and tedious prologue?")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("自从进化成为时尚以来，人类的荣耀就采取了新的形式。我们被告知进化是由一个伟大的目的引导的: 在恐龙和巨型蕨类植物，蜜蜂和野花的整个时代，只有粘液或三叶虫的数百万年，上帝正在准备伟大的高潮。最后，在充裕的时间里，他产生了人，包括尼禄和卡利古拉，希特勒和墨索里尼等标本，其超凡的荣耀证明了漫长的痛苦过程。就我而言，我发现即使是永恒的诅咒也不那么不可思议，当然也不那么荒谬，比这个蹩脚和无能的结论更可笑，我们被要求钦佩为全能的最高努力。如果上帝确实是无所不能的，那么为什么没有如此冗长而乏味的序言，他就不能产生光荣的结果呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("Apart from the question whether Man is really so glorious as the theologians of evolution say he is, there is the further difficulty that life on this planet is almost certainly temporary. The earth will grow cold, or the atmosphere will gradually fly off, or there will be an insufficiency of water, or, as Sir James Jeans genially prophesies, the sun will burst and all the planets will be turned into gas. Which of those will happen first, no one knows; but in any case the human race will ultimately die out. Of course, such an event is of little importance from the point of view of orthodox theology, since men are immortal, and will continue to exist in heaven and hell when none are left on earth. But in that case why bother about terrestrial developments? Those who lay stress on the gradual progress from the primitive slime to Man attach an importance to this mundane sphere which should make them shrink from the conclusion that all life on earth is only a brief interlude between the nebula and the eternal frost, or perhaps between one nebula and another. The importance of Man, which is the one indispensable dogma of the theologians, receives no support from a scientific view of the future of the solar system.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("除了人类是否真的像进化论神学家所说的那样光荣的问题之外，还有一个进一步的困难，那就是这个星球上的生命几乎肯定是暂时的。地球会变冷，或者大气层会逐渐飞走，或者水会不足，或者，正如詹姆斯·金斯爵士和蔼地预言的那样，太阳会爆裂，所有的行星都会变成气体。这些事情中哪一件会先发生，无人知晓；但无论如何，人类终将灭绝。当然，从正统神学的角度来看，这样的事件并不重要，因为人是不朽的，当没有人留在地球上时，他们将继续存在于天堂和地狱中。但在那种情况下，又何必在意地面上的发展呢？那些强调从原始粘液到人类的逐渐进步的人重视这个平凡的领域，这应该使他们从结论中缩小，即地球上的所有生命只是星云和永恒的霜冻之间的短暂插曲，或者在一个星云和另一个星云之间。人的重要性，这是神学家不可或缺的教条之一，没有得到太阳系未来的科学观点的支持。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("There are many other sources of false belief besides self-importance. One of these is love of the marvellous. I knew at one time a scientificallyminded conjurer, who used to perform his tricks before a small audience, and then get them, each separately, to write down what they had seen happen. Almost always they wrote down something much more astonishing than the reality, and usually something which no conjurer could have achieved; yet they all thought they were reporting truly what they had seen with their own eyes. This sort of falsification is still more true of rumours. A tells B that last night he saw Mr.-, the eminent prohibitionist, slightly the worse for liquor; B tells C that A saw the good man reeling drunk, C tells D that he was picked up unconscious in the ditch, D tells E that he is well known to pass out every evening. Here, it is true, another motive comes in, namely malice. We like to think ill of our neighbours, and are prepared to believe the worst on very little evidence. But even where there is no such motive, what is marvellous is readily believed unless it goes against some strong prejudice. All history until the eighteenth century is full of prodigies and wonders which modern historians ignore, not because they are less well attested than facts which the historians accept, but because modern taste among the learned prefers what science regards as probable. Shakespeare relates how on the night before Caesar was killed,")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("除了自我重要性之外，还有许多其他错误信念的来源。其中之一便是对奇迹的热爱。我曾经认识一个有科学头脑的魔术师，他过去常常在一小部分观众面前表演他的把戏，然后让他们每个人分别写下他们看到的事情。他们几乎总是写下比现实更令人惊讶的东西，通常是魔术师无法实现的东西; 然而，他们都认为他们真实地报道了他们亲眼所见的东西。这种捏造更多的是谣言。A告诉B，昨晚他看到了著名的禁酒主义者-先生，酒的情况稍差; B告诉C，A看到好人喝醉了，C告诉D，他在沟里被昏迷了，D告诉E，众所周知，他每天晚上都会昏倒。在这里，这是真的，另一个动机进来，即恶意。我们喜欢认为我们的邻居不好，并准备相信最坏的证据很少。但是，即使没有这样的动机，奇妙的事情也很容易被相信，除非它违背了一些强烈的偏见。直到18世纪，所有的历史都充满了现代历史学家忽视的神童和奇迹，不是因为它们没有历史学家接受的事实得到充分证明，而是因为有学问的现代品味更喜欢科学认为可能的东西。莎士比亚讲述了在凯撒被杀的前一天晚上，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("'A common slave -- you know him well by sight --")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("“一个普通的奴隶 -- 你很了解他 --")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("Held up his left hand, which did flame and burn")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("举起他的左手，火焰和燃烧")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("Like twenty torches join'd, and yet his hand,")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("就像二十个火把加入了，然而他的手，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("Not sensible of fire, remain'd unscorch'd.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("对火不敏感，保持不变。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("Besides -- I have not since put up my sword --")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("此外 -- 我还没有举起我的剑 --")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("Against the Capitol I met a lion,")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("反对国会大厦，我遇到了一头狮子，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("Who glared upon me, and went surly by,")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("谁怒视着我，从我身边走过，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("Without annoying me: and there were drawn")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("不惹我生气：而且那里还画着")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("Upon a heap a hundred ghastly women,")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("在一堆一百个可怕的女人身上，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("Transformed with their fear; who swore they saw")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("他们的恐惧改变了; 谁发誓他们看到了")]

#html.p(id: "en-S045")[#text("Men all in fire walk up and down the streets.'")]

#zh(id: "S045")[#text("所有在火中行走的人在街上走来走去。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("Shakespeare did not invent these marvels; he found them in reputable historians, who are among those upon whom we depend for our knowledge concerning Julius Caesar. This sort of thing always used to happen at the death of a great man or the beginning of an important war. Even so recently as 1914 the \"angels of Mons\" encouraged the British troops. The evidence for such events is very seldom first-hand, and modern historians refuse to accept it-except, of course, where the event is one that has religious importance.")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("莎士比亚没有发明这些奇迹; 他发现他们在著名的历史学家，谁是那些我们依赖于我们对凯撒的知识。这种事情总是发生在伟人去世或重要战争开始时。即使在最近1914，“蒙斯天使” 也鼓励了英军。这些事件的证据很少是第一手的，现代历史学家拒绝接受它-当然，除非该事件具有宗教意义。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("Every powerful emotion has its own myth-making tendency. When the emotion is peculiar to an individual, he is considered more or less mad if he gives credence to such myths as he has invented. But when an emotion is collective, as in war, there is no one to correct the myths that naturally arise. Consequently in all times of great collective excitement unfounded rumours obtain wide credence. In September, 1914, almost everybody in England believed that Russian troops had passed through England on the way to the Western Front. Everybody knew someone who had seen them, though no one had seen them himself.")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("每一种强大的情感都有自己的神话倾向。当一个人特有的情感时，如果他相信他发明的神话，他或多或少会被认为是疯狂的。但是，当一种情绪是集体的，就像在战争中一样，没有人可以纠正自然产生的神话。因此，在所有集体兴奋的时代，毫无根据的谣言都获得了广泛的信任。在1914年9月，几乎每个英国人都认为俄罗斯军队在前往西线的途中经过了英格兰。每个人都知道有人见过他们，虽然没有人见过他们自己。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S048")[#text("This myth-making faculty is often allied with cruelty. Ever since the middle ages, the Jews have been accused of practising ritual murder. There is not an iota of evidence for this accusation, and no sane person who has examined it believes it. Nevertheless it persists. I have met white Russians who were convinced of its truth, and among many Nazis it is accepted without question. Such myths give an excuse for the infliction of torture, and the unfounded belief in them is evidence of the unconscious desire to find some victim to persecute.")]

#zh(id: "S048")[#text("这种制造神话的能力经常与残酷联系在一起。自中世纪以来，犹太人一直被指控从事仪式谋杀。这项指控没有丝毫证据，也没有一个理智的人检查过它。然而，它仍然存在。我遇到过白人俄罗斯人，他们相信它的真实性，在许多纳粹分子中，它毫无疑问地被接受。这些神话为施加酷刑提供了借口，对它们的毫无根据的信念证明了无意识地希望找到一些受害者进行迫害。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S049")[#text("There was, until the end of the eighteenth century, a theory that insanity is due to possession by devils. It was inferred that any pain suffered by the patient is also suffered by the devils, so that the best cure is to make the patient suffer so much that the devils will decide to abandon him. The insane, in accordance with this theory, were savagely beaten. This treatment was tried on King George III when he was mad, but without success. It is a curious and painful fact that almost all the completely futile treatments that have been believed in during the long history of medical folly have been such as caused acute suffering to the patient. When anaesthetics were discovered, pious people considered them an attempt to evade the will of God. It was pointed out, however, that when God extracted Adam's rib He put him into a deep sleep. This proved that anaesthetics are all right for men; women, however, ought to suffer, because of the curse of Eve. In the West votes for women proved this doctrine mistaken, but in Japan, to this day, women in childbirth are not allowed any alleviation through anaesthetics. As the Japanese do not believe in Genesis, this piece of sadism must have some other justification.")]

#zh(id: "S049")[#text("直到18世纪底，有一种理论认为精神错乱是由于魔鬼的占有。据推断，病人遭受的任何痛苦也是魔鬼遭受的，所以最好的治疗方法是让病人遭受如此多的痛苦，魔鬼会决定抛弃他。根据这一理论，疯子被野蛮殴打。乔治三世发疯时曾尝试过这种治疗方法，但没有成功。这是一个奇怪而痛苦的事实，在漫长的医学愚蠢历史中，几乎所有被认为是完全无效的治疗方法都给患者造成了严重的痛苦。当麻醉药被发现时，虔诚的人认为它们是逃避上帝旨意的企图。然而，有人指出，当上帝拔出亚当的肋骨时，他使他进入了深度睡眠。这证明了麻醉剂对男人来说是可以的; 然而，由于夏娃的诅咒，女人应该受苦。在西方，妇女投票证明了这一学说是错误的，但在日本，直到今天，分娩的妇女都不允许通过麻醉剂进行任何缓解。由于日本人不相信《创世纪》，因此这种虐待主义一定有其他理由。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S050")[#text("The fallacies about \"race\" and \"blood,\" which have always been popular, and which the Nazis have embodied in their official creed, have no objective Justification; they are believed solely because they minister to selfesteem and to the impulse toward cruelty. In one form or another, these beliefs are as old as civilization; their forms change, but their essence remains. Herodotus tells how Cyrus was brought up by peasants, in complete ignorance of his royal blood; at the age of twelve his kingly bearing toward other peasant boys revealed the truth. This is a variant of an old story which is found in all Indo-European countries. Even quite modern people say that \"blood will tell.\" It is no use for scientific physiologists to assure the world that there is no difference between the blood of a Negro and the blood of a white man. The American Red Cross, in obedience to popular prejudice, at first, when America became involved in the present war, decreed that no Negro blood should be used for blood transfusion. As a result of an agitation, it was conceded that Negro blood might be used, but only for Negro patients. Similarly, in Germany, the Aryan soldier who needs blood transfusion is carefully protected from the contamination of Jewish blood.")]

#zh(id: "S050")[#text("关于 “种族” 和 “血统” 的谬论一直很流行，纳粹体现在他们的官方信条中，没有客观的理由; 他们被认为仅仅是因为他们服务于自尊和对残忍的冲动。在一种或另一种形式中，这些信仰与文明一样古老; 它们的形式发生了变化，但其本质仍然存在。希罗多德讲述了赛勒斯是如何由农民抚养长大的，完全不知道他的皇室血统; 在十二岁的时候，他对其他农民男孩的王道揭示了真相。这是一个古老故事的变体，在所有印欧语系国家都可以找到。即使是相当现代的人说，“血会告诉。”科学生理学家向世界保证黑人的血和白人的血没有区别是没有用的。美国红十字会，服从大众的偏见，起初，当美国卷入这场战争时，颁布法令，不应该用黑人的血来输血。由于激动，人们承认可以使用黑人血液，但仅适用于黑人患者。同样，在德国，需要输血的雅利安士兵受到精心保护，免受犹太人血液的污染。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S051")[#text("In the matter of race, there are different beliefs in different societies. Where monarchy is firmly established, kings are of a higher race than their subjects. Until very recently, it was universally believed that men are congenitally more intelligent than women; even so enlightened a man as Spinoza decides against votes for women on this ground. Among white men, it is held that white men are by nature superior to men of other colours, and especially to black men; in Japan, on the contrary, it is thought that yellow is the best colour. In Haiti, when they make statues of Christ and Satan, they make Christ black and Satan white. Aristotle and Plato considered Greeks so innately superior to barbarians that slavery is justified so long as the master is Greek and the slave barbarian. The Nazis and the American legislators who made the immigration laws consider the Nordics superior to Slavs or Latins or any other white men. But the Nazis, under the stress of war, have been led to the conclusion that there are hardly any true Nordics outside Germany; the Norwegians, except Quisling and his few followers, have been corrupted by intermixture with Finns and Laps and such. Thus politics are a clue to descent. The biologically pure Nordic loves Hitler, and if you do not love Hitler, that is proof of tainted blood.")]

#zh(id: "S051")[#text("在种族问题上，不同的社会有不同的信仰。在君主制牢固确立的地方，国王的种族比他们的臣民更高。直到最近，人们普遍认为男人比女人先天更聪明; 即使是像斯宾诺莎这样开明的男人，也以此为由决定反对妇女投票。在白人中，人们认为白人天生优于其他肤色的人，尤其是黑人。相反，在日本，人们认为黄色是最好的颜色。在海地，当他们制作基督和撒旦的雕像时，他们把基督变成黑色，撒旦变成白色。亚里士多德和柏拉图认为希腊人天生就比野蛮人优越，只要主人是希腊人而奴隶是野蛮人，奴隶制就是合理的。纳粹和制定移民法的美国立法者认为北欧人优于斯拉夫人或拉丁人或任何其他白人。但是纳粹，在战争的压力下，得出的结论是，德国以外几乎没有真正的北欧人; 挪威人，除了奎斯林和他的几个追随者，已经被芬兰人和圈圈之类的混杂所腐蚀。因此，政治是血统的线索。生物学上纯粹的北欧人喜欢希特勒，如果你不喜欢希特勒，那就是血液污染的证明。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S052")[#text("All this is, of course, pure nonsense, known to be such by every-one who has studied the subject. In schools in America, children of the most diverse origins are subjected to the same educational system, and those whose business it is to measure intelligence quotients and otherwise estimate the native ability of students are unable to make any such racial distinctions as are postulated by the theorists of race. In every national or racial group there are clever children and stupid children. It is not likely that, in the United States, coloured children will develop as successfully as white children, because of the stigma of social inferiority; but in so far as congenital ability can be detached from environmental influence, there is no clear distinction among different groups. The whole conception of superior races is merely a myth generated by the overweening self-esteem of the holders of power. It may be that, some day, better evidence will be forthcoming; perhaps, in time, educators will be able to prove (say) that Jews are on the average more intelligent than gentiles. But as yet no such evidence exists, and all talk of superior races must be dismissed as nonsense.")]

#zh(id: "S052")[#text("这一切当然纯属无稽之谈，凡曾研习此课题者皆知其然。在美国的学校里，各种各样的孩子都受到同样的教育体系的影响，而那些负责衡量智商和估计学生的本地能力的人则无法做出任何种族歧视的假设。种族理论家。在每一个民族或种族群体中，都有聪明的孩子和愚笨的孩子。在美国，由于社会自卑的耻辱，有色儿童不可能像白人儿童一样成功地发展; 但是就先天能力可以脱离环境影响而言，不同群体之间没有明显的区别。优等种族的整个概念仅仅是由权力持有者的自尊心所产生的神话。也许有一天，更好的证据将会出现; 也许，随着时间的推移，教育者将能够证明 (说) 犹太人平均比外邦人更聪明。然而，迄今为止尚无此类证据，所有关于优等种族的言论都应被视为荒谬。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S053")[#text("There is a special absurdity in applying racial theories to the various populations of Europe. There is not in Europe any such thing as a pure race. Russians have an admixture of Tartar blood, Germans are largely Slavonic, France is a mixture of Celts, Germans, and people of Mediterranean race, Italy the same with the addition of the descendants of slaves imported by the Romans. The English are perhaps the most mixed of all. There is no evidence that there is any advantage in belonging to a pure race. The purest races now in existence are the Pygmies, the Hottentots, and the Australian aborigines; the Tasmanians, who were probably even purer, are extinct. They were not the bearers of a brilliant culture. The ancient Greeks, on the other hand, emerged from an amalgamation of northern barbarians and an indigenous population; the Athenians and Ionians, who were the most civilized, were also the most mixed. The supposed merits of racial purity are, it would seem, wholly imaginary.")]

#zh(id: "S053")[#text("将种族理论应用于欧洲的各个族群，本身就带有某种特殊的荒谬性。在欧洲，并不存在所谓纯粹的种族。俄罗斯人有鞑靼血统的混合物，德国人主要是斯拉夫语，法国是凯尔特人，德国人和地中海人种的混合物，意大利也加入了罗马人进口的奴隶后裔。英国人或许是所有民族中血统最为混杂的。没有证据表明属于一个纯粹的种族有任何优势。现在存在的最纯粹的种族是俾格米人，霍腾特人和澳大利亚原住民; 塔斯马尼亚人，他们可能更纯洁，已经灭绝了。他们不是灿烂文化的载体。另一方面，古希腊人是从北方野蛮人和土著居民的融合中崛起的; 雅典人和爱奥尼亚人是最文明的，也是最混杂的。种族纯洁的所谓优点似乎完全是虚构的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S054")[#text("Superstitions about blood have many forms that have nothing to do with race. The objection to homicide seems to have been, originally, based on the ritual pollution caused by the blood of the victim. God said to Cain: \"The voice of thy brother's blood crieth unto me from the ground.\" According to some anthropologists, the mark of Cain was a disguise to prevent Abel's blood from finding him; this appears also to be the original reason for wearing mourning. In many ancient communities no difference was made between murder and accidental homicide; in either case equally ritual ablution was necessary. The feeling that blood defiles still lingers, for example in the Churching of Women and in taboos connected with menstruation. The idea that a child is of his father's \"blood\" has the same superstitious origin. So far as actual blood is concerned, the mother's enters into the child, but not the father's. If blood were as important as is supposed, matriarchy would be the only proper way of tracing descent.")]

#zh(id: "S054")[#text("围绕血的迷信还有许多形式，与种族毫无关系。反对杀人，最初似乎是因为死者的血会带来仪式上的不洁。上帝对该隐说：“你兄弟的血，有声音从地里向我哀告。”据某些人类学家说，该隐的记号是一种伪装，用来防止亚伯的血找到他；这似乎也是穿丧服最初的缘由。在许多古代社会中，谋杀和意外杀人并无区别，两种情况都同样需要举行涤罪仪式。血会使人不洁的观念至今仍有残留，例如产妇产后重返教会的礼仪，以及与月经有关的禁忌。孩子承继父亲“血脉”的观念，也出自同样的迷信。就实际的血而言，进入孩子体内的是母亲的血，而不是父亲的血。假如血真像人们设想的那么重要，那么按母系追溯世系才会是唯一适当的办法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S055")[#text("In Russia, where, under the influence of Karl Marx, people since the revolution have been classified by their economic origin, difficulties have arisen not unlike those of German race theorists over the Scandinavian Nordics. There were two theories that had to be reconciled: on the one hand, proletarians were good and other people were bad; on the other hand, communists were good and other people were bad. The only way of effecting a reconciliation was to alter the meaning of words. A \"proletarian\" came to mean a supporter of the government; Lenin, though born a Prince, was reckoned a member of the proletariat. On the other hand, the word \"kulak,\" which was supposed to mean a rich peasant, came to mean any peasant who opposed collectivization. This sort of absurdity always arises when one group of human beings is supposed to be inherently better than another. In America, the highest praise that can be bestowed on an eminent coloured man after he is safely dead is to say \"he was a white man.\" A courageous woman is called \"masculine\": Macbeth, praising his wife's courage, says:")]

#zh(id: "S055")[#text("在俄国，革命以后，人们受卡尔·马克思影响，按照经济出身被划分为不同类别，由此出现的困难，与德国种族理论家面对斯堪的纳维亚北欧人时遇到的困难颇为相似。有两种理论必须调和：一方面，无产者是好人，其他人是坏人；另一方面，共产党人是好人，其他人是坏人。要使二者协调，唯一的办法就是改变词语的含义。于是，“无产者”变成了政府支持者的意思；列宁虽然生来是个亲王，也被算作无产阶级的一员。另一方面，“富农”本应指富裕的农民，却变成了任何反对集体化的农民。只要认定某一群人天生优于另一群人，这类荒谬就总会出现。在美国，一位杰出的有色人种人士安然去世以后，人们所能给予他的最高赞誉，便是说“他是个白人”。有勇气的女人则被称为“有男子气概”。麦克白赞赏妻子的勇气时说：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S056")[#text("'Bring forth men children only,")]

#zh(id: "S056")[#text("“你只该生育男儿，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S057")[#text("For thy undaunted mettle should compose")]

#zh(id: "S057")[#text("因为你那无畏的气魄所能造就的，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S058")[#text("Nothing but males.'")]

#zh(id: "S058")[#text("应当只有男子。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S059")[#text("All these ways of speaking come of unwillingness to abandon foolish generalizations.")]

#zh(id: "S059")[#text("所有这些说法，都源于人们不愿放弃愚蠢的概括。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S060")[#text("In the economic sphere there are many widespread superstitions. Why do people value gold and precious stones? Not simply because of their rarity: there are a number of elements called \"rare earths\" which are much rarer than gold, but no one will give a penny for them except a few men of science. There is a theory, for which there is much to be said, that gold and gems were valued originally on account of their supposed magical properties. The mistakes of governments in modern times seem to show that this belief still exists among the sort of men who are called \"practical.\" At the end of the last war, it was agreed that Germany should pay vast sums to England and France, and they in turn should pay vast sums to the United States. Everyone wanted to be paid in money rather than goods; the \"practical\" men failed to notice that there is not that amount of money in the world. They also failed to notice that money is no use unless it is used to buy goods. As they would not use it in this way, it did no good to anyone. There was supposed to be some mystic virtue about gold that made it worth while to dig it up in the Transvaal and put it underground again in bank vaults in America. In the end, of course, the debtor countries had no more money, and, since they were not allowed to pay in goods, they went bankrupt. The Great Depression was the direct result of the surviving belief in the magical properties of gold. It is to be feared that some similar superstition will cause equally bad results after the end of the present war.")]

#zh(id: "S060")[#text("在经济领域，存在着许多广泛的迷信。为什么人们重视黄金和宝石？不仅仅是因为它们的稀有性: 有许多被称为 “稀土” 的元素比黄金稀有得多，但是除了少数科学家之外，没有人会为它们付出一分钱。有一种理论，对此有很多话要说，黄金和宝石的价值最初是因为它们被认为是神奇的属性。现代政府的错误似乎表明，这种信念仍然存在于那些被称为 “实际” 的人中间。上次战争结束时，人们一致认为，德国应该向英国和法国支付巨额款项，而他们也应该向美国支付巨额款项。每个人都希望得到金钱而不是商品的报酬; “实际” 的人没有注意到世界上没有那么多的钱。他们也没有注意到，钱如果不用来购买商品，就毫无用处。由于他们不会以这种方式使用它，因此对任何人都没有益处。应该有一些关于黄金的神秘美德，使它值得在德兰士瓦挖出来，并再次将其放在美国银行金库的地下。最后，当然，债务国没有更多的钱，而且，由于不允许他们支付货款，他们破产了。大萧条是对黄金神奇特性的幸存信念的直接结果。令人担忧的是，某种类似的迷信在当前战争结束后，也可能造成同样严重的后果。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S061")[#text("Politics is largely governed by sententious platitudes which are devoid of truth.")]

#zh(id: "S061")[#text("政治在很大程度上受制于那些毫无真理可言的陈词滥调。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S062")[#text("One of the most widespread popular maxims is, \"human nature cannot be changed.\" No one can say whether this is true or not without first defining \"human nature.\" But as used it is certainly false. When Mr. A utters the maxim, with an air of portentous and conclusive wisdom, what he means is that all men everywhere will always continue to behave as they do in his own home town. A little anthropology will dispel this belief. Among the Tibetans, one wife has many husbands, because men are too poor to support a whole wife; yet family life, according to travellers, is no more unhappy than elsewhere. The practice of lending one's wife to a guest is very common among uncivilized tribes. The Australian aborigines, at puberty, undergo a very painful operation which, throughout the rest of their lives, greatly diminishes sexual potency. Infanticide, which might seem contrary to human nature, was almost universal before the rise of Christianity, and is recommended by Plato to prevent over-population. Private property is not recognized among some savage tribes. Even among highly civilized people, economic considerations will override what is called \"human nature.\" In Moscow, where there is an acute housing shortage, when an unmarried woman is pregnant, it often happens that a number of men contend for the legal right to be considered the father of the prospective child, because whoever is judged to be the father acquires the right to share the woman's room, and half a room is better than no room.")]

#zh(id: "S062")[#text("最广为流传的俗语之一是：“人性不可改变。”如果不首先定义 “人性”，谁也不能说这是否是真的。但如使用它肯定是错误的。当A先生以一种预兆和决定性的智慧说出这句格言时，他的意思是，世界各地的所有人都将继续像在自己的家乡一样行事。一点人类学知识就能破除这种观念。在西藏人中，一个妻子有很多丈夫，因为男人太穷了，无法养活一个完整的妻子; 然而，据旅行者说，家庭生活并不比其他地方更不幸福。将妻子借给客人的做法在不文明的部落中非常普遍。澳大利亚原住民，在青春期，经历一个非常痛苦的手术，在他们的余生，大大降低性能力。杀婴似乎与人性相反，在基督教兴起之前几乎是普遍的，柏拉图建议防止人口过多。一些野蛮部落不承认私有财产。即使在高度文明的人中，经济考虑也会凌驾于所谓的 “人性” 之上。在莫斯科，那里的住房严重短缺，当未婚妇女怀孕时，经常发生的情况是，许多男人争夺被视为准孩子父亲的合法权利，因为被判定为父亲的人获得分享妇女房间的权利，半个房间总比没有房间好。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S063")[#text("In fact, adult \"human nature\" is extremely variable, according to the circumstances of education. Food and sex are very general requirements, but the hermits of the Thebaid eschewed sex altogether and reduced food to the lowest point compatible with survival. By diet and training, people can be made ferocious or meek, masterful or slavish, as may suit the educator. There is no nonsense so arrant that it cannot be made the creed of the vast majority by adequate governmental action. Plato intended his Republic to be founded on a myth which he admitted to be absurd, but he was rightly confident that the populace could be induced to believe it. Hobbes, who thought it important that people should reverence the government however unworthy it might be, meets the argument that it might be difficult to obtain general assent to anything so irrational by pointing out that people have been brought to believe in the Christian religion, and, in particular, in the dogma of transubstantiation. If he had been alive now, he would have found ample confirmation in the devotion of German youth to the Nazis.")]

#zh(id: "S063")[#text("事实上，成人的“人性”会因教育环境的不同而呈现出极大的差异性。食物和性是非常普遍的要求，但是Thebaid的隐士完全避免了性行为，并将食物减少到与生存相适应的最低点。通过饮食和训练，人们可以变得凶猛或温顺，精通或奴役，这可能适合教育者。没有任何荒谬之说，只要经过适当的政府干预，便能成为绝大多数人的信条。柏拉图打算将他的共和国建立在一个他承认荒谬的神话之上，但他有理由相信，可以诱使民众相信它。霍布斯认为重要的是，人们应该尊敬政府，无论它多么不值得，他认为，通过指出人们已经被带到基督教信仰中去，可能很难获得对任何如此不合理的事情的普遍同意，特别是在转变的教条中。如果他如今尚在人世，他必将从德国青年对纳粹的狂热追随中得到充分的印证。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S064")[#text("The power of governments over men's beliefs has been very great ever since the rise of large States. The great majority of Romans became Christian after the Roman emperors had been converted. In the parts of the Roman Empire that were conquered by the Arabs, most people abandoned Christianity for Islam. The division of Western Europe into Protestant and Catholic regions was determined by the attitude of governments in the sixteenth century. But the power of governments over belief in the present day is vastly greater than at any earlier time. A belief, however untrue, is important when it dominates the actions of large masses of men. In this sense, the beliefs inculcated by the Japanese, Russian, and German governments are important. Since they are completely divergent, they cannot all be true, though they may well all be false. Unfortunately they are such as to inspire men with an ardent desire to kill one another, even to the point of almost completely inhibiting the impulse of self-preservation. No one can deny, in face of the evidence, that it is easy, given military power, to produce a population of fanatical lunatics. It would be equally easy to produce a population of sane and reasonable people, but many governments do not wish to do so, since such people would fail to admire the politicians who are at the head of these governments.")]

#zh(id: "S064")[#text("自从大国崛起以来，政府对人们信仰的影响一直很大。罗马皇帝皈依后，绝大多数罗马人成为基督徒。在罗马帝国被阿拉伯人征服的部分地区，大多数人放弃了基督教而放弃了伊斯兰教。西欧分为新教和天主教地区是由16世纪政府的态度决定的。但是，在今天，政府对信仰的权力比以往任何时候都要大得多。一种信念，无论多么不真实，当它支配着大批人的行动时，它是重要的。从这个意义上讲，日本，俄罗斯和德国政府灌输的信念很重要。因为它们是完全不同的，所以它们不可能都是真的，尽管它们很可能都是假的。不幸的是，它们会激发人们渴望互相残杀，甚至几乎完全抑制了自我保护的冲动。面对证据，没有人能否认，鉴于军事力量，制造一群狂热的疯子是很容易的。培养一批理智和理性的人同样容易，但许多政府不希望这样做，因为这样的人不会钦佩这些政府首脑的政治家。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S065")[#text("There is one peculiarly pernicious application of the doctrine that human nature cannot be changed. This is the dogmatic assertion that there will always be wars, because we are so constituted that we feel a need of them. What is true is that a man who has had the kind of diet and education that most men have will wish to fight when provoked. But he will not actually fight unless he has a chance of victory. It is very annoying to be stopped by a speed cop, but we do not fight him because we know that he has the overwhelming forces of the State at his back. People who have no occasion for war do not make any impression of being psychologically thwarted. Sweden has had no war since 1814, but the Swedes were, a few years ago, one of the happiest and most contented nations in the world. I doubt whether they are so still, but that is because, though neutral, they are unable to escape many of the evils of war. If political organization were such as to make war obviously unprofitable, there is nothing in human nature that would compel its occurrence, or make average people unhappy because of its not occurring. Exactly the same arguments that are now used about the impossibility of preventing war were formerly used in defence of duelling, yet few of us feel thwarted because we are not allowed to fight duels.")]

#zh(id: "S065")[#text("人性不能改变这一学说有一种特别有害的应用。这是一种教条式的断言，即总会有战争，因为我们是如此的构成，以至于我们感到需要战争。真实的是，一个拥有大多数男人所拥有的饮食和教育的男人，在被激怒时会希望战斗。但是除非他有获胜的机会，否则他实际上不会战斗。被速度警察拦住是非常烦人的，但我们不打他，因为我们知道他背后有国家的压倒性力量。没有战争机会的人不会给人留下任何心理上受挫的印象。瑞典没有战争1814年，但几年前，瑞典人是世界上最幸福、最满足的国家之一。我怀疑他们是否仍然如此，但这是因为，尽管他们是中立的，但他们无法逃脱战争的许多邪恶。如果政治组织使战争明显无利可图，那么人性中就没有任何东西可以强迫战争发生，或者因为战争没有发生而使普通人不高兴。现在使用的关于不可能防止战争的论点，以前用于捍卫决斗，但我们中很少有人感到受挫，因为我们不允许决斗。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S066")[#text("I am persuaded that there is absolutely no limit to the absurdities that can, by government action, come to be generally believed. Give me an adequate army, with power to provide it with more pay and better food than falls to the lot of the average man, and I will undertake, within thirty years, to make the majority of the population believe that two and two are three, that water freezes when it gets hot and boils when it gets cold, or any other nonsense that might seem to serve the interest of the State. Of course, even when these beliefs had been generated, people would not put the kettle in the ice-box when they wanted it to boil. That cold makes water boil would be a Sunday truth, sacred and mystical, to be professed in awed tones, but not to be acted on in daily life. What would happen would be that any verbal denial of the mystic doctrine would be made illegal, and obstinate heretics would be \"frozen\" at the stake. No person who did not enthusiastically accept the official doctrine would be allowed to teach or to have any position of power. Only the very highest officials, in their cups, would whisper to each other what rubbish it all is; then they would laugh and drink again. This is hardly a caricature of what happens under some modern governments.")]

#zh(id: "S066")[#text("我相信，通过政府的行动，人们普遍相信的荒谬是绝对没有限制的。给我一支足够的军队，有能力为它提供比普通人更多的报酬和更好的食物，我将承诺，在三十年内，让大多数人相信二加二等于三，水变热时结冰，变冷时沸腾，或者任何其他似乎符合国家利益的废话。当然，即使产生了这些信念，人们也不会在要煮沸时将水壶放在冰盒中。寒冷使水沸腾将是一个星期天的真理，神圣而神秘，以敬畏的语气宣称，但不能在日常生活中采取行动。将会发生的是，任何对神秘主义的口头否认都将被视为非法，顽固的异教徒将被 “冻结” 在赌注上。没有热情接受官方教义的人将被允许教书或拥有任何权力职位。只有最高级的官员，在他们的杯子里，会互相窃窃私语，这一切都是垃圾; 然后他们会再次大笑和喝酒。这几乎不是对一些现代政府下发生的事情的讽刺。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S067")[#text("The discovery that man can be scientifically manipulated, and that governments can turn large masses this way or that as they choose, is one of the causes of our misfortunes. There is as much difference between a collection of mentally free citizens and a community moulded by modern methods of propaganda as there is between a heap of raw materials and a battleship. Education, which was at first made universal in order that all might be able to read and write, has been found capable of serving quite other purposes. By instilling nonsense it unifies populations and generates collective enthusiasm. If all governments taught the same nonsense, the harm would not be so great. Unfortunately each has its own brand, and the diversity serves to produce hostility between the devotees of different creeds. If there is ever to be peace in the world, governments will have to agree either to inculcate no dogmas, or all to inculcate the same. The former, I fear, is a Utopian ideal, but perhaps they could agree to teach collectively that all public men, everywhere, are completely virtuous and perfectly wise. Perhaps, when the war is over, the surviving politicians may find it prudent to combine on some such programme.")]

#zh(id: "S067")[#text("发现人类可以被科学操纵，政府可以以这种方式或他们选择的方式转变大量群众，这是我们不幸的原因之一。一群精神自由的公民和一个由现代宣传方法塑造的社区之间的区别，就像一堆原材料和一艘战舰之间的区别一样。教育，最初是普遍的，以便所有人都能够读写，已经发现能够服务于其他目的。通过灌输废话，它统一了人口并产生了集体热情。如果所有政府都教同样的废话，危害就不会那么大。不幸的是，每个人都有自己的品牌，多样性会在不同信条的奉献者之间产生敌意。如果世界上有和平，各国政府将不得不同意要么不灌输教条，要么所有人都灌输教条。前者，我担心，是一个乌托邦式的理想，但也许他们可以同意集体教导，所有公共男人，无处不在，完全道德和完全明智。也许，当战争结束时，幸存的政客们可能会发现结合一些这样的计划是明智的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S068")[#text("But if conformity has its dangers, so has nonconformity. Some \"advanced thinkers\" are of the opinion that any one who differs from the conventional opinion must be in the right. This is a delusion; if it were not, truth would be easier to come by than it is. There are infinite possibilities of error, and more cranks take up unfashionable errors than unfashionable truths. I met once an electrical engineer whose first words to me were: \"How do you do? There are two methods of faith-healing, the one practised by Christ and the one practised by most Christian Scientists. I practice the method practised by Christ.\" Shortly afterwards, he was sent to prison for making out fraudulent balance-sheets. The law does not look kindly on the intrusion of faith into this region. I knew also an eminent lunacy doctor who took to philosophy, and taught a new logic which, as he frankly confessed, he had learnt from his lunatics. When he died he left a will founding a professorship for the teaching of his new scientific methods, but unfortunately he left no assets. Arithmetic proved recalcitrant to lunatic logic. On one occasion a man came to ask me to recommend some of my books, as he was interested in philosophy. I did so, but he returned next day saying that he had been reading one of them, and had found only one statement he could understand, and that one seemed to him false. I asked him what it was, and he said it was the statement that Julius Caesar is dead. When I asked him why he did not agree, he drew himself up and said: \"Because I am Julius Caesar.\" These examples may suffice to show that you cannot make sure of being right by being eccentric.")]

#zh(id: "S068")[#text("然而，如果从众存在其危险，那么特立独行也同样如此。一些“思想先进者”认为，凡是与传统观点相左的人必定是正确的。这是一种错觉；若非如此，真理本应比现在更容易获得。错误的可能性是无限的，而且持有非主流错误观点的人，往往多于坚持非主流真理的人。我曾经遇到一位电气工程师，他对我说的第一句话是: “您好？有两种信仰治疗方法，一种是基督所实践的，另一种是大多数基督教科学家所实践的。我实践的是基督所实践的方法。”不久之后，他因编制虚假财务报表而被判处入狱。法律对信仰介入这一领域持否定态度。我还认识一位著名的精神错乱医生，他学到了哲学，并教授了一种新的逻辑，正如他坦率地承认的那样，他是从疯子那里学到的。他去世时留下了一份遗嘱，拟设立一个教授职位以传授其新的科学方法，但遗憾的是，他并未留下任何财产。算术对疯狂的逻辑表现得十分顽固。有一次，一位男士前来请我推荐几本我的书，因为他对哲学很感兴趣。我照做了，但第二天他又来了，说他读了其中一本，却只看懂了一句，而且那句话在他看来还是错的。我问他那是什么，他说那是“尤利乌斯·凯撒已死”这一陈述。当我问他为何不同意时，他挺直了身子，说道：“因为我是尤利乌斯·凯撒。”这些例子或许足以表明，仅仅因为特立独行，并不能确保自己就是正确的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S069")[#text("Science, which has always had to fight its way against popular beliefs, now has one of its most difficult battles in the sphere of psychology.")]

#zh(id: "S069")[#text("科学历来不得不与大众信念作斗争，如今在心理学领域正面临其最为艰难的挑战之一。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S070")[#text("People who think they know all about human nature are always hopelessly at sea when they have to do with any abnormality. Some boys never learn to be what, in animals, is called \"house trained.\" The sort of person who won't stand any nonsense deals with such cases by punishment; the boy is beaten, and when he repeats the offence he is beaten worse. All medical men who have studied the matter know that punishment only aggravates the trouble. Sometimes the cause is physical, but usually it is psychological, and only curable by removing some deep-seated and probably unconscious grievance. But most people enjoy punishing anyone who irritates them, and so the medical view is rejected as fancy nonsense. The same sort of thing applies to men who are exhibitionists; they are sent to prison over and over again, but as soon as they come out they repeat the offense. A medical man who specialized in such ailments assured me that the exhibitionist can be cured by the simple device of having trousers that button up the back instead of the front. But this method is not tried because it does not satisfy people's vindictive impulses.")]

#zh(id: "S070")[#text("那些认为自己对人性了如指掌的人，当他们不得不处理任何异常时，总是无可救药。有些男孩永远学不会成为动物所说的“定点如厕”的样子。那种不会忍受任何废话的人通过惩罚来处理这种案件; 这个男孩被殴打，当他重复罪行时，他被殴打得更糟。所有研究过此事的医务人员都知道，惩罚只会加剧麻烦。有时原因是身体上的，但通常是心理上的，只有消除一些根深蒂固的，可能是无意识的不满才能治愈。但是大多数人喜欢惩罚任何激怒他们的人，因此医学观点被认为是胡说八道。同样的事情也适用于暴露狂的人; 他们一遍又一遍地被送进监狱，但一旦他们出来，他们就会重犯。一位专门研究这类疾病的医生向我保证，暴露狂可以通过简单的装置治愈，即长裤按钮在后面而不是前面。但这种方法并未被采用，因为它无法满足人们的报复心理。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S071")[#text("Broadly speaking, punishment is likely to prevent crimes that are sane in origin, but not those that spring from some psychological abnormality. This is now partially recognized; we distinguish between plain theft, which springs from what may be called rational self-interest, and kleptomania, which is a mark of something queer. And homicidal maniacs are not treated like ordinary murderers. But sexual aberrations rouse so much disgust that it is still impossible to have them treated medically rather than punitively. Indignation, though on the whole a useful social force, becomes harmful when it is directed against the victims of maladies that only medical skill can cure.")]

#zh(id: "S071")[#text("从广义上讲，惩罚可能会阻止起源理智的犯罪，但不会阻止那些源于某种心理异常的犯罪。这一点现在得到了部分承认; 我们区分普通盗窃，这源于所谓的理性自利，还有盗窃癖，这是某种奇怪的东西的标志。而杀人狂魔并不会被当作普通杀人犯来对待。但是性畸变引起了如此多的厌恶，以至于仍然不可能让他们接受医学治疗而不是惩罚性治疗。愤慨虽然总体上是一种有用的社会力量，但当它针对只有医学技能才能治愈的疾病的受害者时，它就变得有害了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S072")[#text("The same sort of thing happens as regards whole nations. During the last war, very naturally, people's vindictive feelings were aroused against the Germans, who were severely punished after their defeat. Now many people are arguing that the Versailles Treaty was ridiculously mild, since it failed to teach a lesson; this time, we are told, there must be real severity. To my mind, we shall be more likely to prevent a repetition of German aggression if we regard the rank and file of the Nazis as we regard lunatics than if we think of them as merely and simply criminals. Lunatics, of course, have to be restrained; we do not allow them to carry firearms. Similarly the German nation will have to be disarmed. But lunatics are restrained from prudence, not as a punishment, and so far as prudence permits we try to make them happy. Everybody recognizes that a homicidal maniac will only become more homicidal if he is made miserable. In Germany at the present day, there are, of course, many men among the Nazis who are plain criminals, but there must also be many who are more or less mad. Leaving the leaders out of account (I do not urge leniency toward them), the bulk of the German nation is much more likely to learn cooperation with the rest of the world if it is subjected to a kind but firm curative treatment than if it is regarded as an outcast among the nations. Those who are being punished seldom learn to feel kindly toward the men who punish them. And so long as the Germans hate the rest of mankind peace will be precarious.")]

#zh(id: "S072")[#text("同样的情况也发生在整个国家层面。在上次战争期间，人们自然而然地对德国人产生了强烈的报复情绪，他们在战败后受到了严厉的惩罚。如今，许多人认为《凡尔赛条约》过于宽大，因为它未能起到应有的警示作用；而这一次，人们普遍主张必须采取真正的严厉措施。在我看来，如果我们把纳粹的普通成员看作是疯子，而不是把他们看作仅仅是罪犯，我们就更有可能防止德国攻击的重演。疯子当然要加以约束；我们不允许他们携带枪支。同样，德国民族也必须解除武装。然而，对疯子的约束并非出于惩罚；在理智允许的范围内，我们仍力求使他们快乐。人人都明白，如果让一个杀人狂变得痛苦不堪，他只会变得更加嗜杀。在今天的德国，有，当然，纳粹中的许多人是普通罪犯，但也必须有许多人或多或少是疯狂的。让领导人置之不理 (我不敦促对他们宽大处理)，如果德国受到一种善良但坚定的治疗，那么大多数德国民族更有可能与世界其他地区学习合作。被视为国家之间的流浪者。受到惩罚的人很少会对他施加惩罚的人产生善意。只要德国人仇恨全人类，和平就将是岌岌可危的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S073")[#text("When one reads of the beliefs of savages, or of the ancient Babylonians and Egyptians, they seem surprising by their capricious absurdity. But beliefs that are just as absurd are still entertained by the uneducated even in the most modern and civilised societies. I have been gravely assured, in America, that people born in March are unlucky and people born in May are peculiarly liable to corns. I do not know the history of these superstitions, but probably they are derived from Babylonian or Egyptian priestly love. Beliefs begin in the higher social strata, and then, like mud in a river, sink gradually downward in the educational scale; they may take 3,000 or 4,000 years to sink all the way. You may find your colored help making some remark that comes straight out of Plato-not the parts of Plato that scholars quote, but the parts where he utters obvious nonsense, such as that men who do not pursue wisdom in this life will be born again as women. Commentators on great philosophers always politely ignore their silly remarks.")]

#zh(id: "S073")[#text("当人们读到野蛮人或古巴比伦人和埃及人的信仰时，他们反复无常的荒谬似乎令人惊讶。但是，即使在最现代和最文明的社会中，同样荒谬的信仰仍然被未受教育的人所接受。在美国，我得到了很大的保证，三月出生的人是不幸的，五月出生的人特别容易受到玉米的影响。我不知道这些迷信的历史，但可能它们源于巴比伦或埃及的神职人员的爱。信仰始于较高的社会阶层，然后像河里的泥土一样，在教育规模上逐渐下沉; 它们可能需要3,000或4,000年才能完全下沉。你可能会发现你的有色人种帮助做出了一些直接来自柏拉图的评论 -- 不是学者引用的柏拉图的部分，而是他说出明显废话的部分，比如在这一生中不追求智慧的男人将作为女人重生。伟大哲学家的评论家总是礼貌地忽略他们的愚蠢言论。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S074")[#text("Aristotle, in spite of his reputation, is full of absurdities. He says that children should be conceived in the Winter, when the wind is in the North, and that if people marry too young the children will be female. He tells us that the blood of females is blacker then that of males; that the pig is the only animal liable to measles; that an elephant suffering from insomnia should have its shoulders rubbed with salt, olive-oil, and warm water; that women have fewer teeth than men, and so on. Nevertheless, he is considered by the great majority of philosophers a paragon of wisdom.")]

#zh(id: "S074")[#text("亚里士多德，尽管他的名声，充满了荒谬。他说，孩子应该在冬天受孕，那时风在北方，如果人们结婚太早，孩子将是女性。他告诉我们，女性的血液比男性的血液更黑; 猪是唯一容易患麻疹的动物; 患有失眠症的大象应该用盐，橄榄油和温水擦拭肩膀; 女人的牙齿比男人少，等等。然而，他被绝大多数哲学家认为是智慧的典范。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S075")[#text("Superstitions about lucky and unlucky days are almost universal. In ancient times they governed the actions of generals. Among ourselves the prejudice against Friday and the number thirteen is very active; sailors do not like to sail on Friday, and many hotels have no thirteenth floor. The superstitions about Friday and thirteen were once believed by those reputed wise; now such men regard them as harmless follies. But probably 2,000 years hence many beliefs of the wise of our day will have come to seem equally foolish. Man is a credulous animal, and must believe something; in the absence of good grounds for belief, he will be satisfied with bad ones.")]

#zh(id: "S075")[#text("关于吉日与凶日的迷信几乎普遍存在。在古代，它们制约着将领的行动。在我们中间，对星期五和十三号的偏见非常活跃; 水手们不喜欢在星期五航行，许多酒店没有十三楼。关于星期五和十三的迷信曾经被那些著名的智者相信; 现在这些人认为他们是无害的愚蠢。但大概2,000年后，我们这个时代的许多智者的信仰也会变得同样愚蠢。人是一种轻信的动物，必须相信一些东西; 在没有好的信仰理由的情况下，他会对坏的信仰感到满意。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S076")[#text("Belief in \"nature\" and what is \"natural\" is a source of many errors. It used to be, and to some extent still is, powerfully operative in medicine. The human body, left to itself, has a certain power of curing itself., small cuts usually heal, colds pass off, and even serious diseases sometimes disappear without medical treatment. But aids to nature are very desirable, even in these cases. Cuts may turn septic if not disinfected, colds may turn to pneumonia, and serious diseases are only left without treatment by explorers and travellers in remote regions, who have no option. Many practices which have come to seem \"natural\" were originally \"unnatural,\" for instance clothing and washing. Before men adopted clothing they must have found it impossible to live in cold climates. Where there is not a modicum of cleanliness, populations suffer from various diseases, such as typhus, from which Western nations have become exempt. Vaccination was (and by some still is) objected to as \"unnatural.\" But there is no consistency in such objections, for no one supposes that a broken bone can be mended by \"natural\" behaviour. Eating cooked food is \"unnatural\"; so is heating our houses. The Chinese philosopher Lao-tse, whose traditional date is about 600 B.C., objected to roads and bridges and boats as \"unnatural,\" and in his disgust at such mechanistic devices left China and went to live among the Western barbarians. Every advance in civilization has been denounced as unnatural while it was recent.")]

#zh(id: "S076")[#text("对 “自然” 和 “自然” 的信仰是许多错误的根源。它过去是，在某种程度上仍然是，在医学上有强大的作用。人体，留给自己，具有一定的治愈自己的力量。，小切口通常会愈合，感冒会消失，甚至严重的疾病有时会在没有医疗的情况下消失。但是，即使在这些情况下，对自然的帮助也是非常可取的。如果不进行消毒，割伤可能会变成败血症，感冒可能会变成肺炎，而偏远地区的探险者和旅行者只能对严重疾病进行治疗，他们别无选择。许多看似 “自然” 的做法最初是 “不自然” 的，例如衣服和洗涤。在人们采用服装之前，他们一定发现不可能生活在寒冷的气候中。在没有一点清洁的地方，人们会患有各种疾病，例如斑疹伤寒，西方国家已免除这些疾病。疫苗接种被 (有些人仍然是) 反对为 “不自然的”。但是这种反对意见没有一致性，因为没有人认为骨折可以通过 “自然” 行为来修补。吃熟食是 “不自然的”; 加热我们的房子也是如此。中国哲学家老子的传统日期约为公元前600年，他反对道路，桥梁和船只是 “不自然的”，并且对这种机械装置感到厌恶，他离开了中国，并与西方野蛮人生活在一起。文明的每一次进步都被谴责为不自然的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S077")[#text("The commonest objection to birth control is that it is against \"nature.\" (For some reason we are not allowed to say that celibacy is against nature; the only reason I can think of is that it is not new.) Malthus saw only three ways of keeping down the population; moral restraint, vice, and misery. Moral restraint, he admitted, was not likely to be practised on a large scale. \"Vice,\" i.e., birth control, he, as a clergyman, viewed with abhorrence. There remained misery. In his comfortable parsonage, he contemplated the misery of the great majority of mankind with equanimity, and pointed out the fallacies of reformers who hoped to alleviate it. Modern theological opponents of birth control are less honest. They pretend to think that God will provide, however many mouths there may be to feed. They ignore the fact that He has never done so hitherto, but has left mankind exposed to periodical famines in which millions died of hunger. They must be deemed to hold-if they are saying what they believe-that from this moment onward God will work a continual miracle of loaves and fishes which He has hitherto thought unnecessary. Or perhaps they will say that suffering here below is of no importance; what matters is the hereafter. By their own theology, most of the children whom their opposition to birth control will cause to exist will go to hell. We must suppose, therefore, that they oppose the amelioration of life on earth because they think it a good thing that many millions should suffer eternal torment. By comparison with them, Malthus appears merciful.")]

#zh(id: "S077")[#text("反对节育最常见的理由是，它违背了“自然”。(由于某种原因，我们不能说独身是违背自然的; 我能想到的唯一原因是它不是新的。)马尔萨斯只看到了三种压低人口的方法; 道德克制，邪恶和痛苦。他承认，道德约束不太可能在大规模范围内得到践行。“副”，即节育，他，作为一个牧师，以憎恶的眼光看待。仍然存在苦难。在他舒适的牧师教区，他平静地考虑了绝大多数人类的痛苦，并指出了改革者的谬论，他们希望减轻这种痛苦。现代神学中反对节育的人则不那么坦诚。他们假装认为上帝会提供，无论有多少张嘴可以喂养。他们忽略了这样一个事实，即他迄今从未这样做过，但却使人类遭受了周期性的饥荒，数百万人死于饥饿。他们必须被视为持有 -- 如果他们说的是他们所相信的 -- 从这一刻起，上帝将继续创造他迄今认为不必要的饼和鱼的奇迹。也许他们会说，下面的苦难并不重要; 重要的是以后。根据他们自己的神学，大多数反对节育的孩子将会下地狱。因此，我们必须假设，他们反对改善地球上的生命，因为他们认为数百万人遭受永恒的折磨是一件好事。与他们相比，马尔萨斯显得仁慈得多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S078")[#text("Women, as the object of our strongest love and aversion, rouse complex emotions which are embodied in proverbial \"wisdom.\"")]

#zh(id: "S078")[#text("女人，作为我们最强烈的爱和厌恶的对象，唤起复杂的情感，这些情感体现在众所周知的 “智慧” 中。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S079")[#text("Almost everybody allows himself or herself some entirely unjustifiable generalization on the subject of woman. Married men, when they generalize on that subject, judge by their wives; women judge by themselves. It would be amusing to write a history of men's views on women. In antiquity, when male supremacy was unquestioned and Christian ethics were still unknown, women were harmless but rather silly, and a man who took them seriously was somewhat despised. Plato thinks it a grave objection to the drama that the playwright has to imitate women in creating his female roles. With the coming of Christianity woman teak on a new part, that of the temptress; but at the same time she was also found capable of being a saint. In Victorian days the saint was much more emphasized than the temptress; Victorian men could not admit themselves susceptible to temptation. The superior virtue of women was made a reason for keeping them out of politics, where, it was held, a lofty virtue is impossible. But the early feminists turned the argument round, and contended that the participation of women would ennoble politics. Since this has turned out to be an illusion, there has been less talk of women's superior virtue, but there are still a number of men who adhere to the monkish view of woman as the temptress. Women themselves, for the most part, think of themselves as the sensible sex, whose business it is to undo the harm that comes of men's impetuous follies. For my part I distrust all generalizations about women, favourable and unfavourable, masculine and feminine, ancient and modern; all alike, I should say, result from paucity of experience.")]

#zh(id: "S079")[#text("几乎每个人在谈论女性时，都会做出一些完全站不住脚的概括性判断。已婚男子在谈论这一话题时，往往以其妻为参照；而女子则以自身为参照。撰写一部关于男性对女性看法的历史，想必会颇为有趣。在古代，当男性至高无上是毋庸置疑的，基督教伦理仍然是未知的，女人是无害的，但相当愚蠢，一个认真对待她们的男人有点被鄙视。柏拉图认为，剧作家在塑造女性角色时不得不模仿女性，这是对戏剧的一个严重质疑。随着基督教妇女柚木的到来，一个新的部分，即妖妇; 但与此同时，她也被发现有能力成为圣人。在维多利亚时代，圣人比妖妇更受重视; 维多利亚时代的男人不能承认自己容易受到诱惑。女性的高尚品德被用作将其排除于政治之外的理由，因为人们认为，在政治领域，崇高的德性是无法实现的。然而，早期的女权主义者扭转了这一论点，认为女性的参与将使政治更加高尚。由于事实证明这是一种幻想，因此很少有人谈论女性的优越美德，但是仍然有许多男人坚持僧侣认为女人是妖妇。在大多数情况下，女性自认为是理性的一方，其职责在于弥补男性一时冲动所造成的种种过失。就我而言，我不相信所有关于女性的概括，无论是有利的还是不利的，男性和女性的，古代和现代的；可以说，这些情况都源于经验的匮乏。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S080")[#text("The deeply irrational attitude of each sex toward women may be seen in novels, particularly in bad novels. In bad novels by men, there is the woman with whom the author is in love, who usually possesses every charm, but is somewhat helpless, and requires male protection; sometimes, however, like Shakespeare's Cleopatra, she is an object of exasperated hatred, and is thought to be deeply and desperately wicked. In portraying the heroine, the male author does not write from observation, but merely objectives his own emotions. In regard to his other female characters, he is more objective, and may even depend upon his notebook; but when he is in love, his passion makes a mist between him and the object of his devotion. Women novelists, also, have two kinds of women in their books. One is themselves, glamorous and kind, and object of lust to the wicked and of love to the good, sensitive, high-souled, and constantly misjudged. The other kind is represented by all other women, and is usually portrayed as petty, spiteful, cruel, and deceitful. It would seem that to judge women without bias is not easy either for men or for women.")]

#zh(id: "S080")[#text("在小说中，尤其是在不良小说中，可以看到每种性别对女性的非理性态度。在男人的烂小说中，有个和作者相爱的女人，她通常拥有各种魅力，但有些无助，需要男性保护; 然而，有时，就像莎士比亚的克娄巴特拉一样，她是一个被愤恨的对象，被认为是极度邪恶的。在描绘女主人公时，男作者不是从观察中写作，而只是针对自己的情感。关于他的其他女性角色，他更客观，甚至可能依赖于他的笔记本; 但是当他恋爱时，他的激情在他和他奉献的对象之间产生了迷雾。女性小说家也有两种女性。一个人是自己，迷人而善良，是对恶人的欲望对象，对善良，敏感，高灵魂，不断被误判的对象。另一种由所有其他女性代表，通常被描绘成小气，恶意，残忍和欺骗。似乎没有偏见地判断女性对男性或女性都不容易。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S081")[#text("Generalizations about national characteristics are just as common and just as unwarranted as generalizations about women. Until 1870, the Germans were thought of as a nation of spectacled professors, evolving everything out of their inner consciousness, and scarcely aware of the outer world, but since 1870 this conception has had to be very sharply revised. Frenchmen seem to be thought of by most Americans as perpetually engaged in amorous intrigue; Walt Whitman, in one of his catalogues, speaks of \"the adulterous French couple on the sly settee.\" Americans who go to live in France are astonished, and perhaps disappointed, by the intensity of family life. Before the Russian Revolution, the Russians were credited with a mystical Slav soul, which, while it incapacitated them for ordinary sensible behavior, gave them a kind of deep wisdom to which more practical nations could not hope to attain. Suddenly everything was changed: mysticism was taboo, and only the most earthly ideals were tolerated. The truth is that what appears to one nation as the national character of another depends upon a few prominent individuals, or upon the class that happens to have power. For this reason, all generalizations on this subject are liable to be completely upset by any important political change.")]

#zh(id: "S081")[#text("关于民族性格的概括，与关于女性的概括一样常见，也同样缺乏根据。1870年以前，德国人被看成一个戴眼镜的教授民族，凡事都从自己的内心意识中推演出来，对外部世界却几乎一无所知；但1870年以后，这种看法不得不被大幅修正。在大多数美国人眼中，法国人似乎永远忙于风流韵事；沃尔特·惠特曼在他的一段列举中，还写到“偷偷坐在双人沙发上通奸的法国男女”。然而，移居法国的美国人却会惊讶于——也许还会失望于——那里家庭生活的浓厚。俄国革命以前，人们认为俄国人具有一种神秘的斯拉夫灵魂：它虽使他们无法作出寻常而明智的行为，却赋予他们一种更务实的民族不可能企及的深刻智慧。忽然之间，一切都变了：神秘主义成了禁忌，只有最世俗的理想才被容许。实际情况是，一个民族眼中另一个民族的所谓民族性格，取决于少数显赫人物，或恰好掌权的那个阶级。因此，这方面的一切概括，都可能被任何重大政治变革彻底推翻。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S082")[#text("To avoid the various foolish opinions to which mankind are prone, no superhuman genius is required. A few simple rules will keep you, not from all error, but from silly error.")]

#zh(id: "S082")[#text("要避免人类容易陷入的种种愚蠢见解，并不需要超人的天才。几条简单的规则就能让你避开愚蠢的错误，虽然还不能让你免于一切错误。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S083")[#text("If the matter is one that can be settled by observation, make the observation yourself. Aristotle could have avoided the mistake of thinking that women have fewer teeth than men, by the simple device of asking Mrs. Aristotle to keep her mouth open while he counted. He did not do so because he thought he knew. Thinking that you know when in fact you don't is a fatal mistake, to which we are all prone. I believe myself that hedgehogs eat black beetles, because I have been told that they do; but if I were writing a book on the habits of hedgehogs, I should not commit myself until I had seen one enjoying this unappetizing diet. Aristotle, however, was less cautious. Ancient and medieval authors knew all about unicorns and salamanders; not one of them thought it necessary to avoid dogmatic statements about them because he had never seen one of them.")]

#zh(id: "S083")[#text("如果一件事可以通过观察来解决，就亲自观察。亚里士多德只要请亚里士多德夫人张开嘴，数一数她的牙齿，就能避免“女人的牙齿比男人少”这个错误。他没有这么做，因为他自以为知道答案。明明不知道，却以为自己知道，是一种致命的错误，而我们人人都容易犯。我自己也相信刺猬吃黑甲虫，因为别人是这么告诉我的；但如果我要写一本讨论刺猬习性的书，那么在亲眼看见一只刺猬津津有味地吃这种不诱人的食物之前，我是不会贸然下断言的。亚里士多德却没有这么谨慎。古代和中世纪的作家，对独角兽和火蜥蜴都知道得一清二楚；没有一个人认为，自己既然从未见过它们，就有必要避免对它们妄下断言。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S084")[#text("Many matters, however, are less easily brought to the test of experience. If, like most of mankind, you have passionate convictions on many such matters, there are ways in which you can make yourself aware of your own bias. If an opinion contrary to your own makes you angry, that is a sign that you are subconsciously aware of having no good reason for thinking as you do. If some one maintains that two and two are five, or that Iceland is on the equator, you feel pity rather than anger, unless you know so little of arithmetic or geography that his opinion shakes your own contrary conviction. The most savage controversies are those about matters as to which there is no good evidence either way. Persecution is used in theology, not in arithmetic, because in arithmetic there is knowledge, but in theology there is only opinion. So whenever you find yourself getting angry about a difference of opinion, be on your guard; you will probably find, on examination, that your belief is going beyond what the evidence warrants.")]

#zh(id: "S084")[#text("然而，许多事情却不太容易通过经验来测试一下。如果，像大多数人类一样，你对许多这样的事情有热情的信念，有一些方法可以让你意识到自己的偏见。如果一个与你自己相反的观点让你生气，这是一个迹象，表明你下意识地意识到没有充分的理由像你一样思考。如果有人坚持认为二加二等于五，或者冰岛在赤道上，你会感到同情而不是愤怒，除非你对算术或地理知识知之甚少，以至于他的观点动摇了你自己相反的信念。最激烈的争论往往发生在那些双方都缺乏确凿证据的议题上。迫害是在神学中使用的，而不是在算术中使用的，因为在算术中有知识，但在神学中只有意见。因此，每当你发现自己对不同的意见感到愤怒时，要保持警惕; 在检查中，你可能会发现你的信念超出了证据的范围。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S085")[#text("A good way of ridding yourself of certain kinds of dogmatism is to become aware of opinions held in social circles different from your own. When I was young, I lived much outside my own country in France, Germany, Italy, and the United States. I found this very profitable in diminishing the intensity of insular prejudice. If you cannot travel, seek out people with whom you disagree, and read a newspaper belonging to a party that is not yours. If the people and the newspaper seem mad, perverse, and wicked, remind yourself that you seem so to them. In this opinion both parties may be right, but they cannot both be wrong. This reflection should generate a certain caution.")]

#zh(id: "S085")[#text("摆脱某些教条主义的一个好方法是意识到社会圈子中不同于你自己的观点。小时候，我长期生活在自己的祖国之外，先后在法国、德国、意大利和美国生活过。我发现这在削弱孤立群体的偏见方面非常有效。如果你不能旅行，寻找你不同意的人，读一份不属于你的政党的报纸。如果人们和报纸看起来既疯狂又乖戾、甚至邪恶，那就提醒自己：在他们眼中，你也是如此。在这一观点中，双方都可能是对的，但不可能双方都是错的。这一反思应当引发某种谨慎态度。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S086")[#text("Becoming aware of foreign customs, however, does not always have a beneficial effect. In the seventeenth century, when the Manchus conquered China, it was the custom among the Chinese for the women to have small feet, and among the Manchus for the men to wear-pigtails. Instead of each dropping their own foolish custom, they each adopted the foolish custom of the other, and the Chinese continued to wear pigtails until they shook off the dominion of the Manchus in the revolution of 1911.")]

#zh(id: "S086")[#text("然而，了解外国习俗并不总是产生积极的影响。在17世纪，当满族征服中国时，中国人的习俗是女人有小脚，满族的男人有辫子。他们没有各自放弃自己的愚蠢习俗，而是各自采用了对方的愚蠢习俗，中国人继续扎着辫子，直到他们在革命1911年中摆脱了满族的统治。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S087")[#text("For those who have enough psychological imagination, it is a good plan to imagine an argument with a person having a different bias. This has one advantage, and only one, as compared with actual conversation with opponents; this one advantage is that the method is not subject to the same limitations of time or space. Mahatma Gandhi deplores railways and steamboats and machinery; he would like to undo the whole of the industrial revolution. You may never have an opportunity of actually meeting any one who holds this opinion, because in Western countries most people take the advantage of modern technique for granted. But if you want to make sure that you are right in agreeing with the prevailing opinion, you will find it a good plan to test the arguments that occur to you by considering what Gandhi might say in refutation of them. I have sometimes been led actually to change my mind as a result of this kind of imaginary dialogue, and, short of this, I have frequently found myself growing less dogmatic and cocksure through realizing the possible reasonableness of a hypothetical opponent.")]

#zh(id: "S087")[#text("对于那些具备足够心理想象力的人来说，设想与一位持有不同偏见的人进行一场辩论，是个不错的做法。与同对手进行实际对话相比，这仅有一个优势；而这一优势就在于，该方法不受时间和空间的同等限制。圣雄甘地痛斥铁路、轮船和机器；他希望让整个工业革命倒转回去。你或许永远没有机会真正遇到持有这种观点的人，因为在西方国家，大多数人早已把现代技术带来的便利视为理所当然。但如果你想确保自己赞同主流观点是正确的，那么一个不错的办法就是，通过思考甘地可能会如何反驳这些论点来测试一下你想到的那些论据。有时，正是通过这种假想的对话，我确实改变了自己的看法；即便未能如此，我也常常因意识到假想对手观点的合理性而变得不那么武断、不再自以为是。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S088")[#text("Be very wary of opinions that flatter your self-esteem. Both men and women, nine times out of ten, are firmly convinced of the superior excellence of their own sex. There is abundant evidence on both sides. If you are a man, you can point out that most poets and men of science are male; if you are a woman, you can retort that so are most criminals. The question is inherently insoluble, but self esteem conceals this from most people. We are all, whatever part of the world we come from, persuaded that our own nation is superior to all others. Seeing that each nation has its characteristic merits and demerits, we adjust our standard of values so as to make out that the merits possessed by our nation are the really important ones, while its demerits are comparatively trivial. Here, again, the rational man will admit that the question is one to which there is no demonstrably right answer. It is more difficult to deal with the self esteem of man as man, because we cannot argue out the matter with some non-human mind. The only way I know of dealing with this general human conceit is to remind ourselves that man is a brief episode in the life of a small planet in a little corner of the universe, and that, for aught we know, other parts of the cosmos may contain beings as superior to ourselves as we are to jellyfish.")]

#zh(id: "S088")[#text("要格外警惕那些迎合你自尊心的观点。无论男女，十有八九都坚信本性更为优越。双方都有充分的证据。如果你是男性，你可以指出大多数诗人和科学家都是男性；如果你是女性，你可以反驳说，大多数罪犯也是男性。这个问题本质上是无解的，但自尊心却使大多数人对此视而不见。无论我们来自世界哪个角落，我们都深信自己的国家优于其他所有国家。鉴于每个民族都有其独特的优缺点，我们会调整自己的价值标准，从而认定本民族所具有的优点才是真正重要的，而其缺点则相对微不足道。在此，理性之人亦会承认，这个问题并无确凿无疑的正确答案。要处理作为“人”的人的自尊问题更为困难，因为我们无法与某种非人类的心智就此事进行辩论。我所知道的处理这种普遍的人类自负的唯一方法是提醒自己，人类是宇宙中一个小角落里一个小星球生命中的短暂插曲，而且，就我们所知，宇宙的其他部分可能包含优于我们自己的生物，就像我们优于水母一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S089")[#text("Other passions besides self-esteem are common sources of error; of these perhaps the most important is fear. Fear sometimes operates directly, by inventing rumors of disaster in war-time, or by imagining objects of terror, such as ghosts; sometimes it operates indirectly, by creating belief in something comforting, such as the elixir of life, or heaven for ourselves and hell for our enemies. Fear has many forms-fear of death, fear of the dark, fear of the unknown, fear of the herd, and that vague generalized fear that comes to those who conceal from themselves their more specific terrors. Until you have admitted your own fears to yourself, and have guarded yourself by a difficult effort of will against their mythmaking power, you cannot hope to think truly about many matters of great importance, especially those with which religious beliefs are concerned. Fear is the main source of superstition and one of the main sources of cruelty. To conquer fear is the beginning of wisdom, in the pursuit of truth as in the endeavour after a worthy manner of life.")]

#zh(id: "S089")[#text("除了自尊之外，其他激情也是错误的常见根源；其中，或许最重要的便是恐惧。恐惧有时直接运作，通过在战时制造灾难的谣言，或者通过想象恐怖的物体，比如鬼魂; 有时它间接地运作，通过创造对安慰的东西的信仰，比如生命的灵药，或者我们自己的天堂和敌人的地狱。恐惧有许多形式 -- 对死亡的恐惧，对黑暗的恐惧，对未知的恐惧，对群体的恐惧，以及那些隐藏自己更具体的恐怖的人所产生的模糊的广义恐惧。除非你对自己承认自己的恐惧，并通过艰难的意志努力来保护自己对抗他们的神话力量，否则你不能指望真正思考许多非常重要的问题，尤其是那些与宗教信仰有关的问题。恐惧是迷信的主要根源，也是残酷的主要根源之一。战胜恐惧，便是智慧的开端，无论是在追求真理的道路上，还是在努力过一种高尚的生活时皆是如此。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S090")[#text("There are two ways of avoiding fear: one is by persuading ourselves that we are immune from disaster, and the other is by the practice of sheer courage. The latter is difficult, and to everybody becomes impossible at a certain point. The former has therefore always been more popular. Primitive magic has the purpose of securing safety, either by injuring enemies, or by protecting oneself by talismans, spells, or incantations. Without any essential change, belief in such ways of avoiding danger survived throughout the many centuries of Babylonian civilization, spread from Babylon throughout the empire of Alexander, and was acquired by the Romans in the course of their absorption of Hellenistic culture. From the Romans it descended to medieval Christendom and Islam. Science has now lessened the belief in magic, but many people place more faith in mascots than they are willing to avow, and sorcery, while condemned by the Church, is still officially a possible sin.")]

#zh(id: "S090")[#text("有两种方法可以克服恐惧：一是说服自己不会遭遇灾难，二是通过培养纯粹的勇气来做到这一点。后者则较为困难，并且在某个阶段对所有人而言都变得不可能。因此，前者一直更受欢迎。原始魔法的目的是通过伤害敌人或通过护身符，咒语或咒语保护自己来确保安全。在没有任何必要的改变的情况下，这种避免危险的方式的信仰在整个巴比伦文明的许多世纪中得以幸存，从巴比伦传播到整个亚历山大帝国，并被罗马人在吸收希腊化文化的过程中获得。它从罗马人那里传承至中世纪的基督教世界和伊斯兰世界。科学现在已经减少了对魔法的信仰，但是许多人对吉祥物的信仰比他们愿意承认的要多，而巫术虽然受到教会的谴责，但仍然是官方可能的罪过。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S091")[#text("Magic, however, was a crude way of avoiding terrors, and, moreover, not a very effective way, for wicked magicians might always prove stronger than good ones. In the fifteenth, sixteenth, and seventeenth centuries, dread of witches and sorcerers led to the burning of hundreds of thousands convicted of these crimes. But newer beliefs, particularly as to the future life, sought more effective ways of combating fear. Socrates on the day of his death (if Plato is to be believed) expressed the conviction that in the next world he would live in the company of the gods and heroes, and surrounded by just spirits who would never object to his endless argumentation. Plato, in his \"Republic,\" laid it down that cheerful views of the next world must be enforced by the State, not because they were true, but to make soldiers more willing to die in battle. He would have none of the traditional myths about Hades, because they represented the spirits of the dead as unhappy.")]

#zh(id: "S091")[#text("魔法，然而，是避免恐怖的一种粗糙的方式，而且，此外，不是一个非常有效的方法，邪恶的魔术师可能总是比好的更强大。在十五、十六和十七世纪，人们对女巫与术士的恐惧导致数十万人因这些罪行而被处以火刑。然而，较新的信仰，尤其是关于来世的信仰，试图寻求更为有效的途径来消除恐惧。苏格拉底在他去世的那天 (如果要相信柏拉图的话) 表示坚信，在下一个世界，他将与神灵和英雄一起生活，并被永远不会反对他无休止的论证的精神所包围。柏拉图在他的 “共和国” 中规定，对下一个世界的乐观看法必须由国家执行，不是因为它们是真实的，而是为了使士兵更愿意在战斗中丧生。他不会有关于冥王的传统神话，因为它们代表了死者的灵魂是不快乐的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S092")[#text("Orthodox Christianity, in the Ages of Faith, laid down very definite rules for salvation. First, you must be baptised; then, you must avoid all theological error; last, you must, before dying, repent of your sins and receive absolution. All this would not save you from purgatory, but it would insure your ultimate arrival in heaven. It was not necessary to know theology. An eminent cardinal stated authoritatively that the requirements of orthodoxy would be satisfied if you murmured on your death-bed: \"I believe all that the Church believes; the Church believes all that I believe.\" These very definite directions ought to have made Catholics sure of finding the way to heaven. Nevertheless, the dread of hell persisted, and has caused, in recent times, a great softening of the dogmas as to who will be damned. The doctrine, professed by many modern Christians, that everybody will go to heaven, ought to do away with the fear of death, but in fact this fear is too instinctive to be easily vanquished. F. W. H. Myers, whom spiritualism had converted to belief in a future life, questioned a woman who had lately lost her daughter as to what she supposed had become of her soul. The mother replied: \"Oh, well, I suppose she is enjoying eternal bliss, but I wish you wouldn't talk about such unpleasant subjects.\" In spite of all that theology can do, heaven remains, to most people, an \"unpleasant subject.\"")]

#zh(id: "S092")[#text("在信仰的时代，东正教为得救制定了非常明确的准则。首先，你必须受洗; 然后，你必须避免所有神学上的错误; 最后，你必须在临死前悔改自己的罪并得到赦免。这一切并不能使你免于炼狱之苦，却能确保你最终抵达天堂。没有必要了解神学。一位著名的红衣主教权威地说，如果你在临终前喃喃地说，正统的要求就会得到满足: “我相信教会所相信的一切; 教会相信我所相信的一切。”这些非常明确的方向应该使天主教徒确信找到通往天堂的道路。然而，对地狱的恐惧依然存在，并且在近来导致了关于哪些人将被诅咒的教义发生了重大缓和。许多现代基督徒宣称每个人都会去天堂的教义，应该消除对死亡的恐惧，但事实上，这种恐惧太本能了，不容易被征服。F.西。H.迈尔斯，他的唯心主义已经转变为对未来生活的信仰，质疑一个最近失去女儿的女人，她认为她的灵魂已经变成了什么。母亲回答: “哦，好吧，我想她正在享受永恒的幸福，但我希望你不要谈论这种不愉快的话题。”尽管神学可以做的一切，但对大多数人来说，天堂仍然是一个 “不愉快的话题”。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S093")[#text("The most refined religions, such as those of Marcus Aurelius and Spinoza, are still concerned with the conquest of fear. The Stoic doctrine was simple: it maintained that the only true good is virtue, of which no enemy can deprive me; consequently, there is no need to fear enemies. The difficulty was that no one could really believe virtue to be the only good, not even Marcus Aurelius, who, as emperor, sought not only to make his subjects virtuous, but to protect them against barbarians, pestilences, and famines. Spinoza taught a somewhat similar doctrine. According to him, our true good consists in indifference to our mundane fortunes. Both these men sought to escape from fear by pretending that such things as physical suffering are not really evil. This is a noble way of escaping from fear, but is still based upon false belief. And if genuinely accepted, it would have the bad effect of making men indifferent, not only to their own sufferings, but also to those of others.")]

#zh(id: "S093")[#text("最为精微的宗教，如马可·奥勒留与斯宾诺莎所代表的那些，依然以克服恐惧为旨归。斯多葛学说很简单: 它坚持认为，唯一真正的好处是美德，没有敌人可以剥夺我; 因此，没有必要害怕敌人。困难的是，没有人能真正相信美德是唯一的好，甚至马可·奥勒留，谁，作为皇帝，不仅寻求使他的臣民贤惠，但保护他们免受野蛮人，瘟疫，和饥荒。斯宾诺莎也提出了某种颇为相似的学说。在他看来，我们真正的善在于对世俗命运的无动于衷。这两位男子都试图通过假装诸如肉体痛苦之类的事物并非真正的恶，来逃避恐惧。这是一种高尚的逃避恐惧的方式，但其根基仍然是错误的信念。而若被真正接受，它将产生一种不良后果，使人不仅对自己的苦难漠不关心，也对他人的苦难漠不关心。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S094")[#text("Under the influence of great fear, almost everybody becomes superstitious. The sailors who threw Jonah overboard imagined his presence to be the cause of the storm which threatened to wreck their ship. In a similar spirit the Japanese, at the time of the Tokyo earthquake took to massacring Koreans and Liberals. When the Romans won victories in the Punic wars, the Carthaginian became persuaded that their misfortunes were due to a certain laxity which had crept into the worship of Moloch. Moloch liked having children sacrificed to him, and preferred them aristocratic; but the noble families of Carthage had adopted the practice of surreptitiously substituting plebeian children for their own offspring. This, it was thought, had displeased the god, and at the worst moments even the most aristocratic children were duly consumed in the fire. Strange to say, the Romans were victorious in spite of this democratic reform on the part of their enemies.")]

#zh(id: "S094")[#text("在巨大的恐惧之下，几乎所有人都会变得迷信。那些将约拿抛入海中的水手们以为，正是他的存在引发了这场足以摧毁他们船只的风暴。同样地，在东京大地震发生时，日本人也大肆屠杀朝鲜人和自由主义者。当罗马人在布匿战争中赢得胜利时，迦太基人被说服，他们的不幸是由于某种松懈而蔓延到对摩洛的崇拜中。Moloch喜欢让孩子牺牲给他，更喜欢他们贵族; 但是迦太基的贵族家庭采用了秘密地用平民儿童代替自己的后代的做法。人们认为，这使上帝不悦，在最糟糕的时刻，即使是最贵族的孩子也被适当地烧死在火中。说来也怪，尽管敌方进行了这项民主改革，罗马人仍然取得了胜利。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S095")[#text("Collective fear stimulates herd instinct, and tends to produce ferocity toward those who are not regarded as members of the herd. So it was in the French Revolution, when dread of foreign armies produced the reign of terror. And it is to be feared that the Nazis, as defeat draws nearer, will increase the intensity of their campaign for exterminating Jews. Fear generates impulses of cruelty, and therefore promotes such superstitious beliefs as seem to justify cruelty. Neither a man nor a crowd nor a nation can be trusted to act humanely or to think sanely under the influence of a great fear. And for this reason poltroons are more prone to cruelty than brave men, and are also more prone to superstition. When I say this, I am thinking of men who are brave in all respects, not only in facing death. Many a man will have the courage to die gallantly, but will not have the courage to say, or even to think, that the cause for which he is asked to die is an unworthy one. Obloquy is, to most men, more painful than death; that is one reason why, in times of collective excitement, so few men venture to dissent from the prevailing opinion. No Carthaginian denied Moloch, because to do so would have required more courage than was required- to face death in battle.")]

#zh(id: "S095")[#text("集体恐惧刺激了群体本能，并倾向于对那些不被视为群体成员的人产生凶猛。在法国大革命中，对外国军队的恐惧产生了恐怖统治。令人担心的是，随着失败的临近，纳粹将会增加他们灭绝犹太人的运动的强度。恐惧产生残酷的冲动，因此促进了似乎为残酷辩护的迷信。在巨大的恐惧的影响下，一个人，一群人或一个国家都不能被信任人道地行动或理智地思考。因此，poltroons比勇敢的人更容易残酷，也更容易迷信。当我这么说的时候，我想到的是在各个方面都勇敢的人，不仅仅是面对死亡。许多人有勇气英勇地死去，但没有勇气说，甚至没有勇气去想，他被要求死的原因是不值得的。对大多数人来说，Obloquy比死亡更痛苦; 这就是为什么在集体兴奋的时候，很少有人敢于反对主流观点。迦太基人没有否认Moloch，因为这样做需要比在战斗中面对死亡所需要的更多的勇气。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S096")[#text("But we have been getting too solemn. Superstitions are not al-ways dark and cruel; often they add to the gaiety of life. I received once a communication from the god Osiris, giving me his telephone number; he lived, at that time, in a suburb of Boston. Although I did not enroll myself among his worshipers, his letter gave me pleasure. I have frequently received letters from men announcing themselves as the Messiah, and urging me not to omit to mention this important fact in my lectures. During prohibition, there was a sect which maintained that the communion service ought to be celebrated in whiskey, not in wine; this tenet gave them a legal right to a supply of hard liquor, and the sect grew rapidly. There is in England a sect which maintains that the English are the lost ten tribes; there is a stricter sect, which maintains that they are only the tribes of Ephraim and Manasseh. Whenever I encounter a member of either of these sects, I profess myself an adherent of the other, and much pleasant argumentation results. I like also the men who study the Great Pyramid, with a view to deciphering its mystical lore. Many great books have been written on this subject, some of which have been presented to me by their authors. It is a singular fact that the Great Pyramid always predicts the history of the world accurately up to the date of publication of the book in question, but after that date it becomes less reliable. Generally the author expects, very soon, wars in Egypt, followed by Armageddon and the coming of Antichrist, but by this time so many people have been recognized as Antichrist that the reader is reluctantly driven to scepticism.")]

#zh(id: "S096")[#text("不过我们最近变得太严肃了。迷信并不总是阴暗而残酷的；它们往往为生活增添几分欢乐。我曾收到过一次来自神奥西里斯的讯息，他给了我他的电话号码；那时，他住在波士顿的一个郊区。尽管我并未将自己列入他的信徒之列，但他的来信仍令我愉悦。我经常收到一些自称是弥赛亚的男士来信，敦促我在演讲中务必提及这一重要事实。在禁酒令期间，有一个教派坚持认为应该用威士忌而不是葡萄酒来庆祝圣餐服务; 这一宗旨赋予了他们提供烈性酒的合法权利，该教派迅速发展。在英格兰有一个教派，认为英国人是失落的十个部落; 有一个更严格的教派，认为他们只是以法莲和玛拿西的部落。每当我遇到这两个教派中的任何一方的成员时，我便自称是另一方的信徒，由此常常引发许多愉快的辩论。我也喜欢那些研究大金字塔、试图解读其神秘奥义的学者。关于这一主题，已经有许多优秀的著作问世，其中一些还由作者亲自赠予于我。这是一个独特的事实，大金字塔总是准确地预测世界的历史，直到有问题的书出版之日，但在那之后，它变得不那么可靠了。一般来说，作者预计，很快，战争在埃及，其次是世界末日和敌基督的到来，但到这个时候，这么多人已经认识到作为敌基督者，读者因而不得不勉强持怀疑态度。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S097")[#text("I admire especially a certain prophetess who lived beside a lake in Northern New York State about the year 1820. She announced to her numerous followers that she possessed the power of walking on water, and that she proposed to do so at 11 o'clock on a certain morning. At the stated time, the faithful assembled in their thousands beside the lake. She spoke to them, saying: \"Are you all entirely persuaded that I can walk on water?\" With one voice they replied: \"We are.\" \"In that case,\" she announced, \"there is not need for me to do so.\" And they all went home much edified.")]

#zh(id: "S097")[#text("我尤其钦佩一位女先知，她大约在1820年前后居住在纽约州北部的一片湖泊旁。她向众多粉丝宣布，自己拥有在水面上行走的超能力，并表示将于某日上午11点当众演示。在指定的时间，成千上万的信徒聚集在湖畔。她对他们说：“你们都真的相信我能在水面上行走吗？”他们异口同声地回答：“我们是。”“既然如此，”她宣布道，“那我就没有必要这么做了。”于是，他们都带着满满的收获回家了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S098")[#text("Perhaps the world would lose some of its interest and variety if such beliefs were wholly replaced by cold science. Perhaps we may allow ourselves to be glad of the Abecedarians, who were so-called because, having rejected all profane learning, they thought it wicked to learn the ABC. And we may enjoy the perplexity of the South American Jesuit who wondered how the sloth could have travelled, since the Flood, all the way from Mount Ararat to Peru-a journey which its extreme tardiness of locomotion rendered almost incredible. A wise man will enjoy the goods of which there is a plentiful supply, and of intellectual rubbish he will find an abundant diet, in our own age as in every other.")]

#zh(id: "S098")[#text("如果这些信念全被冷冰冰的科学取代，世界也许会失去一些趣味和多样性。或许我们不妨为所谓的“字母派”感到高兴：他们之所以得了这个名字，是因为他们排斥一切世俗学问，连学习字母ABC也视为罪恶。我们也可以欣赏那位南美耶稣会士的困惑：洪水以后，树懒究竟怎样一路从阿拉拉特山走到了秘鲁？它行动如此迟缓，这段旅程实在几乎令人难以置信。明智的人会享用供应充足的好东西；至于智识垃圾，无论在我们这个时代，还是在任何别的时代，他都会发现食粮丰盛。")]
